window.VOCAB_CLASSROOM = [{"id":"praeteritum","number":1,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"Präteritum","title_en":"The simple past: for stories and reports","summary":"Tell what happened in writing (*Der Zug kam um 8 Uhr an.*): regular verbs with *-te*, irregular verbs with a new stem, and why Swiss speakers hardly ever say it.","purpose":["The Präteritum is the **simple past**: one word, no helper verb. *Ich **ging** nach Hause.* (I went home.) It means the same as the Perfekt (*Ich **bin** nach Hause **gegangen***). The difference is mainly **where** you use it.","You need it for **reading and writing**: stories, reports, newspaper articles, a CV, an email about what happened. In **conversation** most people use the Perfekt, except for *war*, *hatte* and the modal verbs (*konnte, musste, wollte* …), which are common in speech too."],"logic":["Three kinds of verbs, three patterns: **regular** verbs add *-te* (*machen → machte*), **irregular** verbs change the stem (*gehen → ging*), and **mixed** verbs do both (*denken → dachte*).","**ich** and **er / sie / es** have the same form, with **no ending**: *ich ging, er ging*; *ich machte, sie machte*.","The other persons add the endings *du -(e)st, wir -(e)n, ihr -(e)t, sie / Sie -(e)n*: *du gingst, wir gingen*; *du machtest, wir machten*. Strong verbs whose stem ends in *-t* or *-d* add an *e*: *du fandest, ihr fandet*.","Learn every irregular verb with its three forms: *gehen – ging – gegangen*. You can't work out the Präteritum stem from the infinitive.","German tenses don't show aspect. The Präteritum is **not** the Italian *imperfetto*, the French *imparfait* or the Spanish *imperfecto*: it is used for single, finished events too (*Der Zug **kam** um 8 Uhr **an**.*).","In conversation, use the Perfekt for most verbs. *war*, *hatte* and the modal verbs (*konnte, musste, wollte* …) are normal in the Präteritum in speech too: *Ich **war** krank und **konnte** nicht kommen.*"],"rules":[{"title":"Regular verbs: stem + -te","text":"Take the stem (*machen → mach-*) and add **-te** plus the ending. If the stem ends in **-d** or **-t**, add **-ete** so you can pronounce it: *arbeiten → arbeitete*, *reden → redete*.","table":{"caption":"Präteritum of regular verbs","head":["","machen","arbeiten"],"rows":[["ich","**machte**","**arbeitete**"],["du","machtest","arbeitetest"],["er / sie / es","**machte**","**arbeitete**"],["wir","machten","arbeiteten"],["ihr","machtet","arbeitetet"],["sie / Sie","machten","arbeiteten"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Früher **wohnte** ich in Basel.","en":"I used to live in Basel."},{"de":"Der Techniker **reparierte** den Drucker in zehn Minuten.","en":"The technician repaired the printer in ten minutes."},{"de":"Wir **warteten** eine halbe Stunde auf das Tram.","en":"We waited half an hour for the tram.","note":"*warten* ends in *-t*, so *-ete-*."}],"source":"schwach"},{"title":"Irregular verbs: a new stem, no ending for ich and er","text":"Irregular (strong) verbs change their stem vowel and take **no -te**. *ich* and *er / sie / es* add **nothing** to the new stem; the other persons take the normal endings.","table":{"caption":"Präteritum of irregular verbs","head":["","gehen","kommen","fahren"],"rows":[["ich","**ging**","**kam**","**fuhr**"],["du","gingst","kamst","fuhrst"],["er / sie / es","**ging**","**kam**","**fuhr**"],["wir","gingen","kamen","fuhren"],["ihr","gingt","kamt","fuhrt"],["sie / Sie","gingen","kamen","fuhren"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Am Montag **fuhr** ich mit dem Velo ins Büro.","en":"On Monday I cycled to the office."},{"de":"Die Gäste **kamen** um 19 Uhr.","en":"The guests came at 7 p.m."}],"source":"flexion"},{"title":"Common irregular verbs: learn three forms","text":"The new stem is not predictable, so learn each irregular verb as a set: infinitive, Präteritum, Perfekt.","table":{"caption":"Irregular verbs you need often","head":["infinitive","Präteritum","Perfekt"],"rows":[["bleiben","**blieb**","ist geblieben"],["essen","**ass**","hat gegessen"],["finden","**fand**","hat gefunden"],["geben","**gab**","hat gegeben"],["lesen","**las**","hat gelesen"],["nehmen","**nahm**","hat genommen"],["schlafen","**schlief**","hat geschlafen"],["schreiben","**schrieb**","hat geschrieben"],["sprechen","**sprach**","hat gesprochen"],["treffen","**traf**","hat getroffen"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich **traf** meine Kollegin am Bahnhof.","en":"I met my colleague at the station."},{"de":"Nach dem Essen **schrieb** sie noch zwei E-Mails.","en":"After the meal she wrote two more emails."}],"source":"stammformen"},{"title":"Mixed verbs: -te and a new stem","text":"A few verbs add *-te* like regular verbs but also change the stem like irregular verbs: *denken → **dachte***, *bringen → **brachte***, *kennen → **kannte***, *nennen → **nannte***, *rennen → **rannte***, and *wissen → **wusste***. The Partizip II changes the same way: *gedacht, gebracht, gekannt, gewusst*.","examples":[{"de":"Ich **dachte** lange über das Angebot **nach**.","en":"I thought about the offer for a long time.","note":"*nachdenken* is mixed and separable: *dachte … nach*."},{"de":"Wir **kannten** niemanden in Zürich.","en":"We didn't know anyone in Zurich."},{"de":"Sie **wusste** die Antwort sofort.","en":"She knew the answer straight away."}],"source":"schwach"},{"title":"Modal verbs: -te and no umlaut","text":"Modal verbs add *-te* and **drop the umlaut**: *können → konnte*, *müssen → musste*, *dürfen → durfte*. *mögen* also changes *g* to *ch*: *mochte*. With an umlaut (*könnte, müsste, dürfte*) it is the Konjunktiv II, which means something different.","table":{"caption":"Präteritum of the modal verbs","head":["","können","müssen","dürfen","wollen","sollen","mögen"],"rows":[["ich / er / sie / es","**konnte**","**musste**","**durfte**","**wollte**","**sollte**","**mochte**"],["du","konntest","musstest","durftest","wolltest","solltest","mochtest"],["wir / sie / Sie","konnten","mussten","durften","wollten","sollten","mochten"],["ihr","konntet","musstet","durftet","wolltet","solltet","mochtet"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich **konnte** gestern nicht zum Kurs kommen.","en":"I couldn't come to the class yesterday."},{"de":"Wir **mussten** lange auf den Bus warten.","en":"We had to wait a long time for the bus."},{"de":"Als Kind **mochte** ich keinen Spinat.","en":"As a child I didn't like spinach."}],"source":"stammformen"},{"title":"sein, haben, werden","text":"Learn these three by heart. *haben* is almost regular, but note the double *t*: *hatte*. *werden* has an *-e* even for *ich* and *er*: *wurde*.","table":{"caption":"Präteritum of sein, haben and werden","head":["","sein","haben","werden"],"rows":[["ich","**war**","**hatte**","**wurde**"],["du","warst","hattest","wurdest"],["er / sie / es","**war**","**hatte**","**wurde**"],["wir","waren","hatten","wurden"],["ihr","wart","hattet","wurdet"],["sie / Sie","waren","hatten","wurden"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Gestern **war** ich in Bern.","en":"Yesterday I was in Bern."},{"de":"Der Zug **hatte** zwanzig Minuten Verspätung.","en":"The train was twenty minutes late."},{"de":"Es **wurde** kalt, und wir gingen nach Hause.","en":"It got cold, and we went home."}],"source":"stammformen"},{"title":"Separable verbs: the prefix goes to the end","text":"Separable verbs split in the Präteritum just as in the present: the verb is in position 2, the prefix at the end. In a subordinate clause (with *weil, dass, als* …) the verb goes to the end and is written as one word again.","examples":[{"de":"Der Zug **kam** um 8 Uhr **an**.","en":"The train arrived at 8 o'clock.","note":"*ankommen → kam … an*"},{"de":"Gestern **rief** mich meine Kollegin **an**.","en":"My colleague phoned me yesterday."},{"de":"Wir waren nervös, weil der Zug so spät **ankam**.","en":"We were nervous because the train arrived so late.","note":"Subordinate clause: one word at the end."}],"source":"praeverb"},{"title":"When to use it: writing vs speaking","text":["**Writing:** stories, reports, newspaper articles and texts about the past mostly use the Präteritum.","**Speaking:** in conversation, most people use the Perfekt: *Was **hast** du am Wochenende **gemacht**?*","The more formal the situation, the more Präteritum you hear."],"examples":[{"de":"Am Montag **fuhr** Herr Keller wie immer mit der S-Bahn nach Zürich.","en":"On Monday Mr Keller took the S-Bahn to Zurich as usual.","note":"A written story: Präteritum."},{"de":"Was **hast** du am Samstag **gemacht**? – Ich **bin** wandern **gegangen**.","en":"What did you do on Saturday? – I went hiking.","note":"A conversation: Perfekt."},{"de":"Ich **war** gestern krank und **konnte** nicht arbeiten.","en":"I was ill yesterday and couldn't work.","note":"*war* and the modal verbs are common in conversation too."}],"source":"schrift"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"Swiss German: no Präteritum in dialect","text":["Swiss German, like the other southern (Upper German) dialects, has practically no Präteritum: it uses the Perfekt for every past event. So Swiss speakers rarely say *ging* or *kam*, even when they speak Standard German.","In **written** Standard German, though, the Präteritum is everywhere: in the newspaper, at work, in books. You need to read it fluently and use it in your own texts."],"examples":[{"de":"Ich **bin** gestern in Bern **gewesen**.","en":"I was in Bern yesterday.","note":"What a Swiss speaker typically says."},{"de":"Ich **war** gestern in Bern.","en":"I was in Bern yesterday.","note":"What you would write."}],"source":"praet"},{"title":"-ete after a consonant + n or m","text":"Regular verbs also take **-ete** when the stem ends in a consonant plus *n* or *m*, to make them easier to say: *öffnen → öffnete*, *rechnen → rechnete*, *regnen → es regnete*, *atmen → atmete*. **But** after *r*, *l* or *h* + *n* there is no extra *e*: *lernen → lernte*, *wohnen → wohnte*.","examples":[{"de":"Es **regnete** den ganzen Tag, deshalb blieben wir zu Hause.","en":"It rained all day, so we stayed at home."},{"de":"Die Chefin **öffnete** das Fenster.","en":"The boss opened the window."}],"source":"schwach"},{"title":"Perfekt in writing when it still matters now","text":"Even in a written text, switch to the Perfekt when you want to stress that something in the past still has an effect now.","examples":[{"de":"Die SBB **hat** den Fahrplan **geändert**: Ab Montag fährt der Zug um 7.32 Uhr.","en":"SBB has changed the timetable: from Monday the train leaves at 7.32."}],"source":"gebrauch"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Er gehte nach Hause.","right":"Er **ging** nach Hause.","why":"*gehen* is irregular: new stem, no *-te*. Learners who know English *-ed* tend to add *-te* to every verb, but irregular verbs must be learnt."},{"wrong":"Sie gingt ins Büro.","right":"Sie **ging** ins Büro.","why":"*er / sie / es* has no ending in the Präteritum. The *-t* belongs to the present tense (*sie geht*)."},{"wrong":"Ich denkte an dich.","right":"Ich **dachte** an dich.","why":"*denken* is a mixed verb: *-te* and a new stem, *dachte*."},{"wrong":"Gestern arbeitet sie bis 20 Uhr.","right":"Gestern **arbeitete** sie bis 20 Uhr.","why":"After *-t* or *-d* the past adds *-ete*. *arbeitet* is the present tense."},{"wrong":"Ich könnte gestern nicht kommen.","right":"Ich **konnte** gestern nicht kommen.","why":"Modal verbs drop the umlaut in the Präteritum. *könnte* is the Konjunktiv II (could, would be able to)."},{"wrong":"Ich möchte dich gestern anrufen.","right":"Ich **wollte** dich gestern anrufen.","why":"*möchte* has no past form of its own. For the past, use *wollte*."},{"wrong":"Der Zug ankam um 8 Uhr.","right":"Der Zug **kam** um 8 Uhr **an**.","why":"Separable verbs split in the Präteritum too: the prefix goes to the end."},{"wrong":"Sahst du gestern das Spiel? (im Chat mit einem Freund)","right":"**Hast** du gestern das Spiel **gesehen**?","why":"In English (*Did you see …?*) and Spanish (*¿Viste …?*) the simple past is normal in conversation. In German conversation the Perfekt is more natural; the Präteritum sounds like a book."}],"examples":[{"de":"Die Sitzung **begann** um 9 Uhr und **dauerte** zwei Stunden.","en":"The meeting started at 9 and lasted two hours."},{"de":"Früher **wohnten** wir in Bern, dann **zogen** wir nach Zürich.","en":"We used to live in Bern, then we moved to Zurich."},{"de":"Ich **wusste** nicht, dass der Laden am Sonntag geschlossen war.","en":"I didn't know that the shop was closed on Sunday."},{"de":"Der Chef **brachte** am Freitag Gipfeli für alle **mit**.","en":"On Friday the boss brought croissants for everyone.","note":"*mitbringen* is mixed and separable: *brachte … mit*."},{"de":"Als Kind **wollte** ich Lokführerin werden.","en":"As a child I wanted to become a train driver."},{"de":"Wir **stiegen** in Olten **um** und **kamen** pünktlich in Bern **an**.","en":"We changed trains in Olten and arrived in Bern on time."},{"de":"Es **regnete**, aber wir **hatten** keinen Schirm.","en":"It was raining, but we didn't have an umbrella."},{"de":"Sie **nahm** das Tram und **war** in zehn Minuten im Büro.","en":"She took the tram and was at the office in ten minutes."}],"later":"The Präteritum in the passive (*Die Brücke **wurde** 1950 **gebaut**.*) comes with the passive topic. Old forms you only meet in books (*du kamest*) don't need to be learnt.","selfcheck":{"types":{"form":{"label":"Präteritum form","instruction":"Write the verb in brackets in the Präteritum, in the right person. Write one word.","example":{"q":"Wir ___ am Montag bis 20 Uhr. (arbeiten)","a":"arbeiteten","why":"Regular verb, stem ends in *-t*: *arbeit-ete-*, plus *-n* for *wir*."}},"rewrite":{"label":"Present → past","instruction":"Rewrite the sentence in the Präteritum. Keep the same word order. Write the whole sentence.","example":{"q":"Das Meeting fängt um 9 Uhr an.","a":"Das Meeting fing um 9 Uhr an.","why":"*anfangen* is irregular and separable: *fing … an*."}},"pick":{"label":"Spoken or written?","instruction":"Read the situation (in simple German) and the two versions. Both are correct German. Write **a** or **b** for the one that sounds more natural in that situation.","example":{"q":"Du erzählst deiner Mitbewohnerin, was heute Morgen passiert ist. a) Ich habe meinen Schlüssel verloren. b) Ich verlor meinen Schlüssel.","a":"a","why":"Conversation: Perfekt. The Präteritum *verlor* sounds like a written story."}}},"items":[{"type":"form","q":"Gestern ___ ich bis 19 Uhr im Büro. (bleiben)","a":["blieb"],"why":"Irregular: *bleiben – blieb – geblieben*, no ending for *ich*."},{"type":"form","q":"Die Chefin ___ uns am Morgen eine Mail. (schicken)","a":["schickte"],"why":"Regular: *schick-* + *-te*, no extra ending for *sie*."},{"type":"form","q":"Anna ___ den Bericht kurz vor Mitternacht. (beenden)","a":["beendete"],"why":"Regular, stem ends in *-d*: *-ete*."},{"type":"form","q":"Er ___ die ganze Nacht an die Prüfung. (denken)","a":["dachte"],"why":"Mixed verb: *denken – dachte – gedacht*."},{"type":"form","q":"Wir ___ leider nicht zur Sitzung kommen. (können)","a":["konnten"],"why":"Modal verb: no umlaut in the Präteritum. *könnten* would be the Konjunktiv II."},{"type":"form","q":"Ich ___ nicht, dass das Büro am Montag geschlossen war. (wissen)","a":["wusste"],"why":"*wissen – wusste – gewusst*, no ending for *ich*."},{"type":"form","q":"Ihr ___ gestern sehr spät zu Hause. (sein)","a":["wart"],"why":"*sein* in the Präteritum: *ihr wart*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Der Bus fährt um 7 Uhr ab.","a":["Der Bus fuhr um 7 Uhr ab."],"why":"*abfahren* is irregular and separable: *fuhr … ab*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Ich rufe meine Mutter am Abend an.","a":["Ich rief meine Mutter am Abend an."],"why":"*anrufen – rief an – angerufen*, prefix at the end."},{"type":"pick","q":"Du erzählst einer Kollegin an der Kaffeemaschine von deinem Wochenende. a) Am Samstag bin ich nach Luzern gefahren. b) Am Samstag fuhr ich nach Luzern.","a":["a"],"why":"Conversation: Perfekt. *fuhr* belongs in a written text."},{"type":"pick","q":"Du schreibst eine kurze Geschichte für die Kurszeitung. a) Es hat geregnet, und Lea hat am Bahnhof Bern auf ihren Bruder gewartet. b) Es regnete, und Lea wartete am Bahnhof Bern auf ihren Bruder.","a":["b"],"why":"A written story: Präteritum."},{"type":"pick","q":"Du schreibst einem Freund eine Chatnachricht. a) Sahst du gestern das Spiel? b) Hast du gestern das Spiel gesehen?","a":["b"],"why":"A chat is like conversation: Perfekt. *Sahst du …?* sounds like a book."}]},"sources":[{"id":"praet","title":"grammis: Präteritum (simple past; replaced by the Perfekt in southern dialects)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/terminologie/211"},{"id":"flexion","title":"grammis: Verbflexion nach Tempus und Modus (verb forms by tense and mood)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/4047"},{"id":"schwach","title":"grammis: Schwache Verben (regular verbs, -te / -ete, mixed verbs)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/4073"},{"id":"stammformen","title":"grammis: Verbale Stammformen (verb stems: strong, mixed, modal and auxiliary verbs)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2769"},{"id":"praeverb","title":"grammis: Präverbfügung (separable verbs)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/terminologie/1185"},{"id":"schrift","title":"grammis: Präteritum versus Präsensperfekt (spoken vs written use)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/4206"},{"id":"gebrauch","title":"grammis: Die Vorstellung hat bereits begonnen oder begann bereits? Präteritum or Perfekt","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/3287"}]},{"id":"als-wenn","number":2,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"als / wenn","title_en":"Saying \"when\": als, wenn and wann","summary":"Use *als* for one time in the past, *wenn* for every time and for now or later, and *wann* for questions.","purpose":["English has one word, *when*. German has three: **als**, **wenn** and **wann**. Italian *quando*, French *quand* and Spanish *cuando* also use one word for all three, so the choice is new for almost everyone.","You need it whenever you tell a story from your life (*Als ich in die Schweiz kam, …*), talk about habits (*Wenn ich Frühschicht habe, …*) or ask about a time (*Wann beginnt der Kurs?*)."],"logic":["Ask two questions. **Is it a question about a time?** Then *wann*. **Is it one time in the past?** Then *als*. Everything else is *wenn*.","**als** = **one** event or **one period** in the past: *Als ich ankam, …* (one moment), *Als ich ein Kind war, …* (childhood happened once, even if it lasted years).","**wenn** = **every time**, in the past, present or future, and also **if**: *(Immer) wenn ich nach Bern fahre, …*, *Wenn es morgen regnet, …*","**wann** = **at what time?**, in direct and indirect questions: *Wann kommst du?* *Ich weiss nicht, wann er kommt.*","*als* and *wenn* start a subordinate clause, so the conjugated verb goes to the **end**. If that clause comes first, the main clause **starts with its verb**: *Als ich ankam, **regnete** es.*"],"rules":[{"title":"Overview: als, wenn or wann?","text":"Two things decide: is it **one time** or **every time**, and is it **past** or **present / future**? Questions about a time always take *wann*.","table":{"caption":"Which word for \"when\"?","head":["","past","present / future"],"rows":[["one time","**als**","**wenn**"],["every time (repeated)","**wenn**","**wenn**"],["question: at what time?","**wann**","**wann**"]]},"examples":[{"de":"**Als** ich gestern ins Büro kam, war der Kaffee schon fertig.","en":"When I got to the office yesterday, the coffee was already made.","note":"One time in the past → *als*."},{"de":"**Wenn** ich ins Büro komme, trinke ich zuerst einen Kaffee.","en":"When I get to the office, I have a coffee first.","note":"Every time, present → *wenn*."},{"de":"**Wann** kommst du ins Büro?","en":"When are you coming to the office?","note":"A question → *wann*."}],"source":"temporal"},{"title":"als: one event or one period in the past","text":["Use **als** for something that happened **once** in the past: a moment (*Als das Telefon klingelte, …*) or a period that happened once (*Als ich in Genf wohnte, …*, *Als ich ein Kind war, …*).","The main clause may describe things that happened often during that period: *Als ich ein Kind war, spielte ich jeden Tag Fussball.* The *als*-clause still names one period, so it stays *als*."],"examples":[{"de":"**Als** ich 2019 nach Winterthur zog, kannte ich niemanden.","en":"When I moved to Winterthur in 2019, I didn't know anyone."},{"de":"**Als** ich ein Kind war, spielte ich jeden Tag Fussball.","en":"When I was a child, I played football every day.","note":"*jeden Tag* is in the main clause. Childhood is one period, so the clause takes *als*."},{"de":"Ich war gerade unter der Dusche, **als** das Telefon klingelte.","en":"I was just in the shower when the phone rang."}],"source":"als"},{"title":"wenn: every time, in the past too","text":"For something that happened **again and again** in the past, use **wenn**, not *als*. Words like *immer* (always) or *jedes Mal* (every time) make it clear: *Immer wenn …*, *Jedes Mal, wenn …*","examples":[{"de":"**Immer wenn** ich meine Grossmutter besuchte, backte sie einen Kuchen.","en":"Whenever I visited my grandmother, she baked a cake."},{"de":"**Wenn** wir früher Ferien hatten, fuhren wir ans Meer.","en":"In the past, whenever we had holidays, we went to the seaside."},{"de":"**Jedes Mal, wenn** ich den Zug nahm, hatte er Verspätung.","en":"Every time I took the train, it was late."}],"source":"wenn-temporal"},{"title":"wenn: now and in the future, and = if","text":"In the present and future, **wenn** covers both English *when* and *if*. It works for habits (*Wenn ich im Homeoffice arbeite, …*), for one event that is coming (*Wenn das Paket kommt, …*) and for conditions (*Wenn es regnet, …*).","examples":[{"de":"**Wenn** ich im Homeoffice arbeite, mache ich mittags einen Spaziergang.","en":"When I work from home, I go for a walk at lunchtime."},{"de":"**Wenn** das Paket morgen kommt, rufe ich dich an.","en":"When the parcel arrives tomorrow, I'll call you.","note":"English chooses between *when* and *if*. German uses *wenn* for both."},{"de":"**Wenn** es morgen regnet, bleiben wir zu Hause.","en":"If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay at home."}],"source":"wenn"},{"title":"wann: questions, direct and indirect","text":["**wann** asks for a time. In a direct question, the verb comes in position 2: *Wann **fährt** der Bus?*","In an indirect question, after *Weisst du, …*, *Ich weiss nicht, …* or *Können Sie mir sagen, …*, it is still *wann*, and the verb goes to the **end**."],"examples":[{"de":"**Wann** fährt der nächste Bus nach Wil?","en":"When does the next bus to Wil leave?"},{"de":"Weisst du, **wann** das Meeting **beginnt**?","en":"Do you know when the meeting starts?"},{"de":"Ich weiss noch nicht, **wann** ich Ferien **nehme**.","en":"I don't know yet when I'll take my holidays."}],"source":"w"},{"title":"Word order: verb at the end, then verb first","text":"In the *als*- or *wenn*-clause, the conjugated verb goes to the **end**. The clause can come first or second. If it comes first, it fills position 1, so the main clause **starts with its verb**.","table":{"caption":"The same sentence in both orders","head":["","part 1","part 2"],"rows":[["clause first","**Als** ich nach Hause **kam**,","**war** niemand da."],["clause second","Niemand **war** da,","**als** ich nach Hause **kam**."]]},"examples":[{"de":"**Wenn** ich müde **bin**, **trinke** ich einen Kaffee.","en":"When I'm tired, I drink a coffee."},{"de":"Ich trinke einen Kaffee, **wenn** ich müde **bin**.","en":"I drink a coffee when I'm tired."},{"de":"**Als** wir in Genf **angekommen sind**, **hat** es geregnet.","en":"When we arrived in Geneva, it was raining.","note":"With two verb parts, the conjugated one (*sind*) goes last."}],"source":"integration"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"als also means \"than\"","text":["After a comparative (*grösser, älter, mehr, lieber*), **als** means *than*. This has nothing to do with time.","With the plain form, use **so … wie** (as … as): *so gross wie*. In everyday speech, especially in the south, you also hear *grösser wie*. In writing and in the exam, use *grösser als*."],"examples":[{"de":"Zürich ist **grösser als** Bern.","en":"Zurich is bigger than Bern."},{"de":"Ich trinke **lieber** Tee **als** Kaffee.","en":"I prefer tea to coffee."},{"de":"Das neue Büro ist **so gross wie** das alte.","en":"The new office is as big as the old one."}],"source":"vergleich"},{"title":"als + noun means \"as\"","text":"*als* + a noun, with no verb, names a role: *als Lehrerin* (as a teacher), *als Kind* (as a child). *Als Kind* is a short way of saying *Als ich ein Kind war*.","examples":[{"de":"Sie arbeitet **als** Pflegefachfrau im Spital.","en":"She works as a nurse in the hospital."},{"de":"**Als** Kind wohnte ich in Lugano.","en":"As a child, I lived in Lugano.","note":"*Als Kind* fills position 1, so the verb (*wohnte*) comes next."}],"source":"als"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Wenn ich gestern nach Hause kam, war niemand da.","right":"**Als** ich gestern nach Hause kam, war niemand da.","why":"One time in the past → *als*. English *when*, French *quand*, Italian *quando* and Spanish *cuando* don't make this split, so learners reach for *wenn*."},{"wrong":"Immer als ich Kopfweh hatte, nahm ich eine Tablette.","right":"Immer **wenn** ich Kopfweh hatte, nahm ich eine Tablette.","why":"*immer* shows it happened again and again. Repeated → *wenn*, even in the past."},{"wrong":"Wenn kommst du?","right":"**Wann** kommst du?","why":"A question about a time takes *wann*. English *when?*, French *quand ?*, Italian *quando?* and Spanish *¿cuándo?* use the same word as in statements, so learners mix them up."},{"wrong":"Ich weiss nicht, wenn der Zug fährt.","right":"Ich weiss nicht, **wann** der Zug fährt.","why":"An indirect question about a time is still *wann*, just as English \"I don't know when\" is a question."},{"wrong":"Ich weiss nicht, wenn er heute kommt. (= whether)","right":"Ich weiss nicht, **ob** er heute kommt.","why":"English *if*, French *si*, Italian *se* and Spanish *si* also mean \"whether\". German uses *ob* for \"whether\"; *wenn* means \"if\" only in a condition."},{"wrong":"Als ich war ein Kind, …","right":"Als ich ein Kind **war**, …","why":"*als* and *wenn* send the conjugated verb to the end of their clause."},{"wrong":"Als ich ein Kind war, ich wohnte in Lugano.","right":"Als ich ein Kind war, **wohnte ich** in Lugano.","why":"After the clause, the main clause starts with its verb. English and French put the subject first here, so this is a common transfer mistake."},{"wrong":"Wo ich klein war, …","right":"**Als** ich klein war, …","why":"In Swiss German dialect you often hear *wo* in this job. In Standard German, write and say *als*."}],"examples":[{"de":"**Als** ich in die Schweiz kam, sprach ich kein Wort Deutsch.","en":"When I came to Switzerland, I didn't speak a word of German."},{"de":"**Als** meine Tochter geboren wurde, wohnten wir noch in Basel.","en":"When my daughter was born, we were still living in Basel."},{"de":"**Wenn** ich Frühschicht habe, stehe ich um fünf Uhr auf.","en":"When I'm on the early shift, I get up at five."},{"de":"Früher sind wir jedes Wochenende in die Berge gefahren, **wenn** das Wetter schön war.","en":"We used to go to the mountains every weekend when the weather was nice."},{"de":"**Wenn** du am Bahnhof bist, schreib mir kurz.","en":"When you're at the station, send me a quick message."},{"de":"**Wann** hast du morgen Feierabend?","en":"When do you finish work tomorrow?"},{"de":"Können Sie mir sagen, **wann** die Post am Samstag schliesst?","en":"Can you tell me when the post office closes on Saturday?"},{"de":"Mein Bruder ist drei Jahre jünger **als** ich.","en":"My brother is three years younger than me."}],"later":"Other time words for clauses follow the same word order (verb at the end) and come in later lessons: *nachdem* (after), *bevor* (before), *während* (while), *seit* (since), *bis* (until), *sobald* (as soon as). *als ob* (as if) also comes later.","selfcheck":{"types":{"choose":{"label":"als, wenn or wann?","instruction":"Write the missing word: *als*, *wenn* or *wann*. Write one word only. Ask yourself: is it a question about a time, one time in the past, or every time / now / later? In one sentence the gap is a comparison (\"than\").","example":{"q":"___ ich gestern nach Hause kam, war niemand da.","a":"Als","why":"*gestern* = one time in the past → *als*."}},"join":{"label":"Join the sentences","instruction":"Join the two sentences with *als* or *wenn*. Choose the word yourself. Put the verb of the *als*/*wenn*-clause at the end. You may start with *als*/*wenn* (then the main clause starts with its verb) or put the clause second. Keep the tenses as they are. Write the whole sentence.","example":{"q":"Ich kam ins Büro. Das Telefon klingelte. (ein Moment, gestern)","a":"Als ich ins Büro kam, klingelte das Telefon.","why":"One time in the past → *als*. *kam* goes to the end, and the main clause starts with its verb (*klingelte*)."}}},"items":[{"type":"choose","q":"___ ich 18 war, habe ich die Autoprüfung gemacht.","a":["Als"],"why":"Being 18 happened once, in the past → *als*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Jedes Mal, ___ ich in Italien war, habe ich meine Grosseltern besucht.","a":["wenn"],"why":"*Jedes Mal* = repeated, so *wenn*, even in the past."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ beginnt der Deutschkurs?","a":["Wann"],"why":"A question about a time → *wann*."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ du morgen Zeit hast, können wir zusammen essen.","a":["Wenn"],"why":"Future, and also a condition (if) → *wenn*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Weisst du, ___ der letzte Zug nach Luzern fährt?","a":["wann"],"why":"An indirect question about a time → *wann*, with the verb (*fährt*) at the end."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ wir letzten Winter in Davos waren, hat es die ganze Woche geschneit.","a":["Als"],"why":"One stay, last winter → one time in the past → *als*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Mein Handy klingelt immer, ___ ich in einer Sitzung bin.","a":["wenn"],"why":"*immer*, present → every time → *wenn*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Meine Schwester ist zwei Jahre älter ___ ich.","a":["als"],"why":"After a comparative (*älter*), *als* means \"than\"."},{"type":"join","q":"Ich war 20. Ich zog nach Zürich.","a":["Als ich 20 war, zog ich nach Zürich.","Ich zog nach Zürich, als ich 20 war.","Als ich nach Zürich zog, war ich 20.","Ich war 20, als ich nach Zürich zog."],"why":"Being 20 was one time in the past → *als*. *war* goes to the end; if the clause comes first, the main clause starts with *zog*."},{"type":"join","q":"Es regnet. Ich nehme das Tram.","a":["Wenn es regnet, nehme ich das Tram.","Immer wenn es regnet, nehme ich das Tram.","Wenn es regnet, nehme ich immer das Tram.","Ich nehme das Tram, wenn es regnet.","Ich nehme immer das Tram, wenn es regnet."],"why":"Present, every time it rains → *wenn*. *regnet* goes to the end; after the clause, the main clause starts with *nehme*."}]},"sources":[{"id":"als","title":"grammis connector dictionary: als (temporal subjunctor, verb at the end; also als + noun)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/407056"},{"id":"wenn","title":"grammis connector dictionary: wenn (temporal and conditional, verb at the end)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/407100"},{"id":"wenn-temporal","title":"grammis: Konditionalsätze (includes temporal wenn for repeated and for single events)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2101"},{"id":"temporal","title":"grammis: Temporalsätze (time clauses with als, während, nachdem, bevor, seit)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2104"},{"id":"w","title":"grammis: Termsatzeinleitende W-Elemente (wann, wo, warum … in indirect questions)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2093"},{"id":"integration","title":"grammis: Integration der Nebensätze (a subordinate clause in first position)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1948"},{"id":"vergleich","title":"grammis: Grösser als oder grösser wie? (als and wie in comparisons)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/134"}]},{"id":"plusquamperfekt","number":3,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"Plusquamperfekt","title_en":"The past perfect: what had happened before","summary":"Say what had already happened before something else in the past (*Als ich ankam, war der Zug schon abgefahren.*), above all after *nachdem*.","purpose":["The Plusquamperfekt shows that something happened **before** another event in the past. It takes you one step further back: *Als ich am Bahnhof ankam, **war** der Zug schon **abgefahren**.* (When I got to the station, the train had already left.)","It works like English *had* + past participle (*had left*). You find it mostly in stories and reports, next to the Präteritum, and after *nachdem* (after)."],"logic":["Form: take the Perfekt and put the helper verb into the Präteritum: *habe gegessen → **hatte** gegessen*, *bin gefahren → **war** gefahren*.","Same verb bracket as the Perfekt: *hatte / war* in position 2, the Partizip II at the end. In a subordinate clause both go to the end: *…, weil ich den Bus **verpasst hatte**.*","Think of a timeline: **Plusquamperfekt** (earlier) → **Präteritum / Perfekt** (later) → **now**.","Use it only when you jump back in time. If you tell events in the order they happened, the Präteritum or the Perfekt is enough.","Typical signal words: *nachdem* (after), *schon* / *bereits* (already), *vorher* / *zuvor* (before that), *noch nie* (never before).","Watch the umlaut: *ich **hatte** das gemacht* (I had done that) is the Plusquamperfekt; *ich **hätte** das gemacht* (I would have done that) is the Konjunktiv II."],"rules":[{"title":"Form: hatte / war + Partizip II","text":"The Plusquamperfekt is the Präteritum of *haben* or *sein* plus the Partizip II of the main verb.","table":{"caption":"Plusquamperfekt with hatte and war","head":["","arbeiten (haben)","fahren (sein)"],"rows":[["ich","**hatte** gearbeitet","**war** gefahren"],["du","hattest gearbeitet","warst gefahren"],["er / sie / es","**hatte** gearbeitet","**war** gefahren"],["wir","hatten gearbeitet","waren gefahren"],["ihr","hattet gearbeitet","wart gefahren"],["sie / Sie","hatten gearbeitet","waren gefahren"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich **hatte** den Termin **vergessen**.","en":"I had forgotten the appointment."},{"de":"Wir **waren** schon nach Hause **gegangen**.","en":"We had already gone home."}],"source":"plqpf"},{"title":"hatte or war? The same choice as in the Perfekt","text":["If a verb forms its Perfekt with *sein*, its Plusquamperfekt takes **war**. If it takes *haben*, it takes **hatte**.","**war:** movement from A to B (*fahren, gehen, kommen, einsteigen*), a change of state (*einschlafen, aufwachen, passieren*), and *sein, bleiben, werden*.","**hatte:** verbs with an accusative object (*essen, kaufen, schreiben*), most other verbs (*arbeiten, schlafen, warten*), and modal verbs."],"examples":[{"de":"Die Kinder **waren** schon **eingeschlafen**.","en":"The children had already fallen asleep.","note":"*einschlafen* is a change of state: *ist eingeschlafen*, so *war*."},{"de":"Er **hatte** schlecht **geschlafen**.","en":"He had slept badly.","note":"*schlafen* takes *haben*: *hat geschlafen*, so *hatte*."},{"de":"Ich **war** noch nie in Genf **gewesen**.","en":"I had never been to Geneva."}],"source":"hilfsverb"},{"title":"Meaning: one step further back","text":"The Plusquamperfekt places an event **before** a moment that is already in the past. It is used above all next to events told in the Präteritum.","examples":[{"de":"Lisa **hatte** ihr Handy zu Hause **vergessen**, deshalb konnte sie nicht anrufen.","en":"Lisa had forgotten her phone at home, so she couldn't call."},{"de":"Der Film **hatte** schon **angefangen**, als wir ins Kino kamen.","en":"The film had already started when we got to the cinema."}],"source":"praetperf"},{"title":"nachdem: the earlier event","text":["The *nachdem*-clause describes what happened **first**. Its verb goes to the end. When the *nachdem*-clause comes first, the main clause starts with the verb.","If the main clause is in the **past** (Präteritum, or Perfekt in speech), the *nachdem*-clause takes the **Plusquamperfekt**.","If the main clause is in the **present**, the *nachdem*-clause takes the **Perfekt**."],"table":{"caption":"Tenses with nachdem","head":["","nachdem-clause (earlier)","main clause (later)"],"rows":[["in the past","Plusquamperfekt: *gegessen hatte*","Präteritum: *ging* (speech: *bin gegangen*)"],["in the present","Perfekt: *gegessen habe*","Präsens: *gehe*"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Nachdem ich die E-Mail **geschrieben hatte**, ging ich nach Hause.","en":"After I had written the email, I went home."},{"de":"Nachdem sie in Olten **umgestiegen war**, las sie die Zeitung.","en":"After she had changed trains in Olten, she read the paper."},{"de":"Nachdem wir **angekommen waren**, sind wir ins Hotel gegangen.","en":"After we had arrived, we went to the hotel.","note":"Spoken: main clause in the Perfekt."},{"de":"Nachdem ich **gegessen habe**, gehe ich spazieren.","en":"After I've eaten, I'll go for a walk.","note":"Present main clause: Perfekt after *nachdem*."}],"source":"temporal"},{"title":"bevor: the bevor-clause is the later event","text":"With *bevor* (before), it's the other way round: the *bevor*-clause happens **later**. Usually both clauses take the same past tense. You can use the Plusquamperfekt in the main clause to stress that it was over before the later event.","examples":[{"de":"Bevor ich ins Büro fuhr, **trank** ich einen Kaffee.","en":"Before I drove to the office, I had a coffee."},{"de":"Bevor sie nach Zürich zog, **hatte** sie drei Jahre in Basel **gewohnt**.","en":"Before she moved to Zurich, she had lived in Basel for three years."}],"source":"temporal"},{"title":"schon, vorher, zuvor: a clear step back","text":"German has no fixed sequence of tenses: you choose the tense by meaning. When *schon*, *vorher* or *zuvor* points to a time **before** a past moment, use the Plusquamperfekt.","examples":[{"de":"Ich machte mir keine Sorgen, weil ich solche Schmerzen schon vorher **gehabt hatte**.","en":"I didn't worry, because I had had pain like that before."},{"de":"Als die Chefin kam, **hatten** wir die Präsentation schon **vorbereitet**.","en":"When the boss came, we had already prepared the presentation."}],"source":"zeitenfolge"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"Swiss speech: ich habe gegessen gehabt","text":["Swiss German and other southern dialects have practically no Präteritum, so speakers build the earlier past from the Perfekt: *ich habe gegessen gehabt* instead of *ich hatte gegessen*. You'll hear it in Switzerland, even in Standard German.","This double form is not part of the standard tense system and is rare in written texts. In writing, use **hatte / war** + Partizip II."],"examples":[{"de":"Ich habe schon gegessen gehabt, als du angerufen hast.","en":"I had already eaten when you called.","note":"Spoken in Switzerland; avoid it in writing."},{"de":"Ich **hatte** schon **gegessen**, als du anriefst.","en":"I had already eaten when you called.","note":"Written Standard German."}],"source":"doppel"},{"title":"hatte gehabt and war gewesen are correct","text":"The Plusquamperfekt of *haben* and *sein* themselves is normal Standard German: *hatte … gehabt*, *war … gewesen*. Don't mix them up with the Swiss double forms.","examples":[{"de":"Ich **hatte** an dem Tag keine Zeit **gehabt**.","en":"I hadn't had any time that day."},{"de":"Er **war** eine Woche krank **gewesen**.","en":"He had been ill for a week."}],"source":"kontrast"},{"title":"Events in order don't need it","text":"When you tell events in the order they happened, each one simply follows the last. The Präteritum (or Perfekt) is enough.","examples":[{"de":"Ich **stand** um 6 Uhr **auf**, **duschte** und **fuhr** ins Büro.","en":"I got up at 6, had a shower and went to the office."}],"source":"zeitenfolge"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Nachdem ich gegessen habe, ging ich ins Bett.","right":"Nachdem ich gegessen **hatte**, ging ich ins Bett.","why":"The main clause is in the past (*ging*), so the earlier *nachdem*-event takes the Plusquamperfekt. *gegessen habe* fits only with a present main clause (*…, gehe ich ins Bett*)."},{"wrong":"Gestern hatte ich im Homeoffice gearbeitet. (du erzählst nur, was du gemacht hast)","right":"Gestern **habe** ich im Homeoffice **gearbeitet**.","why":"The Plusquamperfekt needs a later past event to look back from. Italian, French and Spanish use the pluperfect more freely, for example *te l'avevo detto / je te l'avais dit / te lo había dicho* (I told you so). German normally uses the Perfekt there: *Ich habe es dir doch gesagt!*"},{"wrong":"Als ich ankam, hatte der Zug schon abgefahren.","right":"Als ich ankam, **war** der Zug schon **abgefahren**.","why":"*abfahren* is movement: Perfekt with *sein* (*ist abgefahren*), so *war*."},{"wrong":"Ich war den Bericht schon geschrieben.","right":"Ich **hatte** den Bericht schon **geschrieben**.","why":"*schreiben* has an accusative object: Perfekt with *haben*, so *hatte*."},{"wrong":"Nachdem ich hatte gegessen, ging ich ins Bett.","right":"Nachdem ich **gegessen hatte**, ging ich ins Bett.","why":"*nachdem* sends the verb to the end: Partizip II first, then *hatte*. English says *after I had eaten*, which leads to the wrong order."},{"wrong":"Nachdem wir gegessen hatten, wir gingen spazieren.","right":"Nachdem wir gegessen hatten, **gingen wir** spazieren.","why":"After a *nachdem*-clause, the main clause starts with the verb. English and French put the subject first here, which makes this a common trap."},{"wrong":"Nachdem dem Essen gingen wir spazieren.","right":"**Nach** dem Essen gingen wir spazieren.","why":"*nachdem* joins two clauses; *nach* + dative goes with a noun. English *after* does both jobs."},{"wrong":"Ich habe schon gegessen gehabt, als du anriefst. (in einem E-Mail)","right":"Ich **hatte** schon **gegessen**, als du anriefst.","why":"The double Perfekt belongs to Swiss and southern German speech. In written Standard German, use *hatte / war* + Partizip II."}],"examples":[{"de":"Als ich am Bahnhof ankam, **war** der Zug schon **abgefahren**.","en":"When I got to the station, the train had already left."},{"de":"Nachdem wir das Projekt **abgeschlossen hatten**, gingen wir zusammen essen.","en":"After we had finished the project, we went out for a meal together."},{"de":"Sie war müde, weil sie die ganze Nacht nicht **geschlafen hatte**.","en":"She was tired because she hadn't slept all night."},{"de":"Ich **hatte** meinen Pass zu Hause **vergessen** und musste zurückfahren.","en":"I had forgotten my passport at home and had to go back."},{"de":"Die Sitzung **hatte** schon **begonnen**, als ich ins Zimmer kam.","en":"The meeting had already begun when I came into the room."},{"de":"Bis letzten Sommer **war** er noch nie mit dem Glacier Express **gefahren**.","en":"Until last summer he had never travelled on the Glacier Express."},{"de":"Nachdem es den ganzen Tag **geregnet hatte**, schien am Abend endlich die Sonne.","en":"After it had rained all day, the sun finally came out in the evening."},{"de":"Wir **hatten** einen Tisch **reserviert**, aber das Restaurant war geschlossen.","en":"We had booked a table, but the restaurant was closed."}],"later":"The Plusquamperfekt in the passive (*Das Haus **war** renoviert **worden**.*) and with modal verbs (*Ich **hatte** nicht kommen **können**.*) come later, at B2.","selfcheck":{"types":{"aux":{"label":"hatte or war?","instruction":"Write the right form of *hatte* or *war* for the gap. Write one word. Check the person: *wir hatten*, *wir waren*.","example":{"q":"Ich ___ den Film schon gesehen.","a":"hatte","why":"*sehen* forms its Perfekt with *haben* (*habe gesehen*), so *hatte gesehen*."}},"story":{"label":"Tell the story","instruction":"The items are sentences from one short written story about Sara. Put the verb in brackets into the right tense: **Präteritum** for the main events, **Plusquamperfekt** for what happened before them, **Perfekt** for what people say in «quotes». Write only what goes in the gap (one or two words).","example":{"q":"Paul war hungrig, weil er den ganzen Tag nichts ___. (essen)","a":"gegessen hatte","why":"Not eating came before being hungry: Plusquamperfekt. After *weil* both parts go to the end, *hatte* last."}},"join":{"label":"Join with nachdem","instruction":"Join the two sentences with *nachdem*. The first sentence happened first: put it in the *nachdem*-clause in the Plusquamperfekt. Keep the second one in the Präteritum and leave out *dann*. Write the whole sentence and start with *Nachdem*.","example":{"q":"Er duschte. Dann frühstückte er.","a":"Nachdem er geduscht hatte, frühstückte er.","why":"*duschen* takes *haben*: *geduscht hatte* at the end. The main clause starts with the verb."}}},"items":[{"type":"aux","q":"Als ich zur Haltestelle kam, ___ der Bus schon abgefahren.","a":["war"],"why":"*abfahren* is movement: *ist abgefahren*, so *war abgefahren*."},{"type":"aux","q":"Tom ___ sein Portemonnaie zu Hause vergessen, deshalb konnte er nicht bezahlen.","a":["hatte"],"why":"*vergessen* has an accusative object: *hat vergessen*, so *hatte vergessen*."},{"type":"aux","q":"Wir ___ gerade ins Tram eingestiegen, als es zu regnen begann.","a":["waren"],"why":"*einsteigen* is movement: *sind eingestiegen*, so *wir waren*."},{"type":"aux","q":"Er war müde, weil er schlecht geschlafen ___.","a":["hatte"],"why":"*schlafen* takes *haben* (*hat geschlafen*). Only *einschlafen* takes *sein*."},{"type":"aux","q":"Sie ___ schon zweimal in Bern gewesen, bevor sie dort eine Stelle fand.","a":["war"],"why":"*sein* forms its Perfekt with *sein*: *ist gewesen*, so *war gewesen*."},{"type":"story","q":"Am Freitag ___ Sara erst um 22 Uhr nach Hause. (kommen)","a":["kam"],"why":"A main event of the story: Präteritum, *kommen – kam*."},{"type":"story","q":"Sie war sehr müde, weil sie vorher zwölf Stunden ___. (arbeiten)","a":["gearbeitet hatte"],"why":"The work came before the tiredness: Plusquamperfekt, both parts at the end after *weil*."},{"type":"story","q":"Vor der Haustür merkte sie, dass sie ihren Schlüssel im Büro ___. (vergessen)","a":["vergessen hatte"],"why":"She forgot the key before she noticed it: Plusquamperfekt."},{"type":"story","q":"Sie ___ ihre Mitbewohnerin an. (anrufen)","a":["rief"],"why":"The next main event: Präteritum. *anrufen* splits: *rief … an*."},{"type":"story","q":"Sara sagte: «Ich stehe draussen, weil ich meinen Schlüssel ___.» (vergessen)","a":["vergessen habe"],"why":"Sara is speaking about now, so the Perfekt, as in conversation."},{"type":"story","q":"Nachdem die Mitbewohnerin die Tür ___, ging Sara sofort ins Bett. (öffnen)","a":["geöffnet hatte"],"why":"*nachdem* + past main clause: Plusquamperfekt. *öffnen* takes *haben*."},{"type":"join","q":"Wir assen zu Mittag. Dann gingen wir spazieren.","a":["Nachdem wir zu Mittag gegessen hatten, gingen wir spazieren."],"why":"*essen* takes *haben*: *gegessen hatten* for *wir*. Then the main clause starts with the verb: *gingen wir*."},{"type":"join","q":"Sie stieg in Olten aus. Dann wartete sie auf den Bus.","a":["Nachdem sie in Olten ausgestiegen war, wartete sie auf den Bus."],"why":"*aussteigen* is movement: *ausgestiegen war*. Then *wartete sie*."}]},"sources":[{"id":"plqpf","title":"grammis: Plusquamperfekt (form: Präteritum of haben or sein + Partizip II)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/vggf/2228?termini=term"},{"id":"praetperf","title":"grammis: Präteritumperfekt (the Plusquamperfekt: before a past moment)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/terminologie/192"},{"id":"hilfsverb","title":"grammis: Ich bin geschwommen oder ich habe geschwommen? Perfekt with sein or haben","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/86"},{"id":"perfektformen","title":"grammis: Perfektformen (Perfekt and Plusquamperfekt share the choice of haben or sein)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/vggf/2226?termini=term"},{"id":"temporal","title":"grammis: Temporalsätze (nachdem, bevor, als)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2104"},{"id":"zeitenfolge","title":"grammis: Gibt es im Deutschen eine normierte Zeitenfolge? (choosing tenses by meaning)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/165"},{"id":"kontrast","title":"grammis: Das Präteritumperfekt (examples incl. war gewesen)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/4181"},{"id":"doppel","title":"grammis: Warum haben wir das Plusquamperfekt, wenn es nie benutzt wird? (incl. double forms)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/6706"},{"id":"sued","title":"grammis: Präteritum versus Präsensperfekt (southern double forms: ich habe gegessen gehabt)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/4206"}]},{"id":"relativsatz","number":4,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"Relativpronomen und Relativsatz","title_en":"Relative clauses: the colleague who …, the town where …","summary":"Describe a person or thing more precisely with a relative clause (*die Kollegin, **mit der** ich arbeite*), and use *wo* and *was* in the right places.","purpose":["A relative clause adds information about a noun **without starting a new sentence**: *Das ist die Kollegin. Ich arbeite mit ihr.* becomes *Das ist die Kollegin, **mit der** ich arbeite.* (That's the colleague I work with.)","You use it to say **which** person or thing you mean, or to add a detail. You'll need it in descriptions, emails, job ads (*Wir suchen eine Person, **die** gern im Team arbeitet.*) and in the B1 exam."],"logic":["The relative clause usually comes **right after the noun** it describes. It starts with a **relative pronoun**: *der, die, das* and their case forms.","Ask two questions. **Which noun?** It gives the gender and number (masculine, feminine, neuter, plural). **What job does the pronoun do inside the relative clause?** That gives the case (nominative, accusative, dative, genitive).","The forms are the same as the definite article, with three exceptions: **denen** (dative plural) and **dessen / deren** (genitive, *whose*).","A relative clause is a subordinate clause: the conjugated verb goes to the **end**, and **commas** mark where the clause starts and ends.","German **never** leaves out the relative pronoun. English can say *the man I met*; German must say *der Mann, **den** ich getroffen habe*."],"rules":[{"title":"The forms of the relative pronoun","text":"Start from the definite article. Only the dative plural and the genitive are different: **denen**, **dessen**, **deren**. In the table these are marked in bold.","table":{"caption":"Relative pronouns","head":["","masculine","neuter","feminine","plural"],"rows":[["nominative","der","das","die","die"],["accusative","den","das","die","die"],["dative","dem","dem","der","**denen**"],["genitive","**dessen**","**dessen**","**deren**","**deren**"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Das ist das Büro, **das** wir gemietet haben.","en":"That's the office we've rented."},{"de":"Die Kunden, **denen** wir das Angebot geschickt haben, haben schon geantwortet.","en":"The customers we sent the offer to have already replied."}],"source":"formen"},{"title":"Gender and number from the noun, case from the relative clause","text":["The **noun** before the comma decides **der, die, das** or plural. The **verb or preposition inside the relative clause** decides the case.","So the case of the noun in the main clause doesn't matter. Look only at the relative clause: is the pronoun the subject (nominative), the object of a verb like *sehen, anrufen* (accusative), or the object of a verb like *helfen, danken, geben jemandem* (dative)?"],"examples":[{"de":"Das ist der Kollege, **der** neben mir sitzt.","en":"That's the colleague who sits next to me.","note":"Nominative: *der Kollege sitzt neben mir*."},{"de":"Das ist der Kollege, **den** ich gestern angerufen habe.","en":"That's the colleague I called yesterday.","note":"Accusative: *ich habe den Kollegen angerufen*."},{"de":"Das ist der Kollege, **dem** ich beim Umzug geholfen habe.","en":"That's the colleague I helped with his move.","note":"Dative: *ich habe dem Kollegen geholfen*."},{"de":"Ich kenne den Mann, **der** in der Kantine arbeitet.","en":"I know the man who works in the canteen.","note":"*den Mann* is accusative in the main clause, but in the relative clause the man is the subject: **der**."}],"source":"attribut"},{"title":"The verb goes to the end","text":"A relative clause works like a *weil*- or *dass*-clause: the conjugated verb stands **last**. With a modal or a perfect tense, the conjugated verb comes after the infinitive or Partizip II.","examples":[{"de":"Ich suche eine Stelle, die mir Spass **macht**.","en":"I'm looking for a job that I enjoy."},{"de":"Die Wohnung, die wir gestern besichtigt **haben**, ist leider zu teuer.","en":"Unfortunately, the flat we viewed yesterday is too expensive."},{"de":"Das ist ein Kurs, den man auch online besuchen **kann**.","en":"That's a course you can also take online."}],"source":"rel"},{"title":"Commas around the relative clause","text":"Put a comma **before** the relative clause. If the main clause continues after it, put a comma **after** it too. If a preposition starts the relative clause, the comma goes before the preposition: *die Firma**,** bei der ich arbeite*.","examples":[{"de":"Der Zug**,** den ich jeden Morgen nehme**,** hatte heute Verspätung.","en":"The train I take every morning was late today."},{"de":"Die Leute**,** mit denen ich wandern gehe**,** kommen aus dem Aargau.","en":"The people I go hiking with come from Aargau."}],"source":"komma"},{"title":"Preposition + relative pronoun","text":"If the verb needs a preposition (*arbeiten bei, sprechen über, sich freuen auf*), the preposition goes **first**, in front of the pronoun. The **preposition** then decides the case: *mit* + dative, *für* + accusative, and so on.","examples":[{"de":"Das ist die Kollegin, **mit der** ich das Projekt mache.","en":"That's the colleague I'm doing the project with.","note":"*mit* + dative, feminine: *der*."},{"de":"Die Firma, **bei der** ich arbeite, hat 50 Mitarbeitende.","en":"The company I work for has 50 employees."},{"de":"Der Kurs, **für den** ich mich angemeldet habe, beginnt im Januar.","en":"The course I've signed up for starts in January.","note":"*für* + accusative, masculine: *den*."},{"de":"Das sind die Nachbarn, **von denen** ich dir erzählt habe.","en":"Those are the neighbours I told you about.","note":"*von* + dative plural: *denen*."}],"source":"rel"},{"title":"dessen and deren: whose","text":["**dessen** refers back to a masculine or neuter noun, **deren** to a feminine or plural noun. The choice depends on the **owner** (the noun before the comma), not on the thing owned.","The noun after *dessen / deren* has **no article**: *der Nachbar, dessen Hund …* (never *dessen der Hund*)."],"examples":[{"de":"Ich habe einen Nachbarn, **dessen** Hund jede Nacht bellt.","en":"I have a neighbour whose dog barks every night."},{"de":"Das ist die Kollegin, **deren** Mann in Basel arbeitet.","en":"That's the colleague whose husband works in Basel."},{"de":"Eltern, **deren** Kinder in die Krippe gehen, müssen früh aufstehen.","en":"Parents whose children go to day care have to get up early."}],"source":"attribut"},{"title":"wo for places","text":"After a noun for a place, you can use **wo** instead of preposition + pronoun: *die Stadt, **wo** ich wohne* = *die Stadt, **in der** ich wohne*. Both are correct. *wo* is simpler and common in speech.","examples":[{"de":"Kennst du ein Restaurant, **wo** man gut Fondue essen kann?","en":"Do you know a restaurant where you can get good fondue?"},{"de":"Das ist das Dorf, **in dem** meine Grosseltern wohnen.","en":"That's the village where my grandparents live.","note":"*wo* is also correct here: *das Dorf, wo …*"}],"source":"wo"},{"title":"was after alles, etwas, nichts, das and after a whole clause","text":["Use **was** after the pronouns **alles, etwas, nichts** (*das* is possible here too, but much rarer) and after **das** meaning \"the thing(s)\".","Also use **was** when the relative clause comments on the **whole main clause**, not on one noun: *…, **was** mich freut* (…, which makes me happy)."],"examples":[{"de":"**Alles, was** du für den Kurs brauchst, findest du im Intranet.","en":"You'll find everything you need for the course on the intranet."},{"de":"Gibt es **etwas, was** ich für dich tun kann?","en":"Is there anything I can do for you?"},{"de":"Ich kaufe nur **das, was** ich wirklich brauche.","en":"I only buy what I really need."},{"de":"Am Freitag haben wir frei, **was** mich sehr freut.","en":"We have Friday off, which makes me very happy.","note":"*was* refers to the whole first clause."}],"source":"was"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"Place names: only wo","text":"After a place name without an article, like a town (*Luzern*) or most countries (*Italien*), use **wo**. Preposition + pronoun doesn't work here. With an article it does: *in der Schweiz, **in der** …*","examples":[{"de":"Ich arbeite in Luzern, **wo** auch meine Schwester wohnt.","en":"I work in Lucerne, where my sister lives too.","note":"Not: *Luzern, in dem …*"}],"source":"wo"},{"title":"das Buch, was …: spoken, not standard","text":["In speech you often hear *was* after a neuter noun: *das Buch, **was** ich lese*. This counts as non-standard. In writing and in the exam, use **das** after a noun: *das Buch, **das** ich lese*.","After a neuter adjective used as a noun, especially a superlative, **was** is normal: *das Beste, **was** …* (the best thing that …)."],"examples":[{"de":"Das ist das Beste, **was** mir dieses Jahr passiert ist.","en":"That's the best thing that's happened to me this year."},{"de":"Das Geschenk, **das** du mir gegeben hast, gefällt mir sehr.","en":"I really like the present you gave me.","note":"After the noun *das Geschenk*: **das**, not *was*."}],"source":"dasoderwas"},{"title":"The relative clause can sit inside the main clause","text":"If the noun is at the start, the relative clause comes right after it, and the main clause continues after the second comma. Keep the relative clause next to its noun. Only the last part of the verb may stand in between: *Ich habe den Film gesehen, **den** du mir empfohlen hast.* (I've seen the film you recommended to me.)","examples":[{"de":"Die Sitzung, **die** um 9 Uhr beginnt, dauert zwei Stunden.","en":"The meeting that starts at 9 lasts two hours."}],"source":"rel"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Der Mann ich gestern getroffen habe, ist Arzt.","right":"Der Mann, **den** ich gestern getroffen habe, ist Arzt.","why":"English can drop the relative pronoun (*the man I met*). German never can."},{"wrong":"die Kollegin, die ich geholfen habe","right":"die Kollegin, **der** ich geholfen habe","why":"*helfen* takes the dative: feminine dative is *der*. In English, French, Italian and Spanish \"help\" takes a direct object (*que j'ai aidée, che ho aiutato, a la que ayudé*), so learners pick the accusative *die*."},{"wrong":"Ich kenne den Mann, den in der Kantine arbeitet.","right":"Ich kenne den Mann, **der** in der Kantine arbeitet.","why":"The case comes from the relative clause, not the main clause. Here the man is the subject of *arbeitet*: nominative."},{"wrong":"die Kunden, mit die ich spreche","right":"die Kunden, mit **denen** ich spreche","why":"Dative plural is *denen*, not *die* or *den*."},{"wrong":"das Buch, was ich lese","right":"das Buch, **das** ich lese","why":"After a noun, use *der, die, das*. *was* follows *alles, etwas, nichts, das*, a superlative like *das Beste*, or a whole clause."},{"wrong":"die Wohnung, die wir haben gemietet","right":"die Wohnung, die wir gemietet **haben**","why":"The conjugated verb goes to the end of the relative clause. English, French, Italian and Spanish keep the normal word order here."},{"wrong":"die Firma, die ich arbeite","right":"die Firma, **bei der** ich arbeite","why":"The preposition can't go missing or move to the end (English *the company I work for*). It goes first: *bei der*."},{"wrong":"die Kollegin, dessen Mann in Basel arbeitet","right":"die Kollegin, **deren** Mann in Basel arbeitet","why":"*dessen / deren* follow the owner (*die Kollegin*, feminine), not the thing owned. Italian *il cui* and Spanish *cuyo* agree with the thing owned, so this trap is common."}],"examples":[{"de":"Wie heisst die Frau, **die** am Empfang arbeitet?","en":"What's the name of the woman who works at reception?"},{"de":"Das ist der Laptop, **den** ich vom Geschäft bekommen habe.","en":"That's the laptop I got from work."},{"de":"Der Zug, **mit dem** ich nach Bern fahre, ist immer voll.","en":"The train I take to Bern is always full."},{"de":"Die Kinder, **denen** ich Nachhilfe gebe, sind sehr motiviert.","en":"The children I tutor are very motivated."},{"de":"Ich habe eine Nachbarin, **deren** Tochter in Lausanne studiert.","en":"I have a neighbour whose daughter studies in Lausanne."},{"de":"Das Team, **für das** ich arbeite, ist sehr international.","en":"The team I work for is very international."},{"de":"Wir wohnen in einem Quartier, **wo** es viele Spielplätze gibt.","en":"We live in a neighbourhood where there are lots of playgrounds."},{"de":"Alles, **was** im Kühlschrank ist, darfst du essen.","en":"You can eat everything that's in the fridge."},{"de":"Der Bus hatte zwanzig Minuten Verspätung, **was** in der Schweiz selten ist.","en":"The bus was twenty minutes late, which is rare in Switzerland."}],"later":"Free relative clauses with **wer** and **was** that have no noun before them (*Wer zu spät kommt, muss warten.* – Whoever comes late has to wait. *Was du sagst, stimmt.* – What you say is true.), the formal relative pronoun **welcher** (*der Kollege, welcher …*) and the genitive form **derer** come at B2.","selfcheck":{"types":{"fill":{"label":"Fill the gap","instruction":"Write the missing relative word: *der, die, das, den, dem, denen, dessen, deren*, or *was* or *wo*. Write one word. Look at the noun before the comma for gender and number, and at the relative clause for the case.","example":{"q":"Der Kurs, ___ ich besuche, ist am Abend.","a":"den","why":"*der Kurs* is masculine, and *besuchen* takes the accusative: *ich besuche den Kurs*."}},"prep":{"label":"Preposition + pronoun","instruction":"The preposition is given in brackets. Write the preposition and the relative pronoun, in that order (two words).","example":{"q":"Die Firma, ___ ich arbeite, ist in Winterthur. (bei)","a":"bei der","why":"*bei* + dative, *die Firma* is feminine: *bei der*."}},"join":{"label":"Join the sentences","instruction":"Join the two sentences into one. Turn the second sentence into a relative clause and put it right after the noun it describes. Write the whole new sentence.","example":{"q":"Ich habe einen Bruder. Er wohnt in Genf.","a":"Ich habe einen Bruder, der in Genf wohnt.","why":"*Er* is the subject, so nominative masculine: *der*. The verb *wohnt* goes to the end."}}},"items":[{"type":"fill","q":"Der Mann, ___ dort am Fenster steht, ist mein Chef.","a":["der"],"why":"The man is the subject of *steht*: nominative masculine, *der*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Der Mann, ___ ich gestern angerufen habe, ist mein Chef.","a":["den"],"why":"*anrufen* takes the accusative: *ich habe den Mann angerufen*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Der Mann, ___ ich die Unterlagen gegeben habe, ist mein Chef.","a":["dem"],"why":"*geben* + dative for the person: *ich habe dem Mann die Unterlagen gegeben*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Die Kunden, ___ wir eine E-Mail geschickt haben, haben noch nicht geantwortet.","a":["denen"],"why":"*schicken* + dative for the person, plural: *denen*, not *die*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Ich kenne die Frau, ___ Auto vor unserem Haus steht.","a":["deren"],"why":"The owner is *die Frau* (feminine): *deren*. *Auto* has no article."},{"type":"fill","q":"Das ist das Haus, ___ meine Grosseltern gebaut haben.","a":["das"],"why":"After the noun *das Haus*: *das*, accusative neuter. Not *was*, because there's a noun."},{"type":"fill","q":"Das ist das Dorf, ___ meine Grosseltern wohnen.","a":["wo","in dem"],"why":"A place: *wo*. *in dem* is also correct."},{"type":"fill","q":"Es gibt nichts, ___ ich lieber esse als Rösti.","a":["was","das"],"why":"After *nichts*: *was*. *das* is also correct, but much rarer."},{"type":"prep","q":"Die Kollegen, ___ ich zu Mittag esse, sind sehr nett. (mit)","a":["mit denen"],"why":"*mit* + dative plural: *denen*."},{"type":"prep","q":"Das Projekt, ___ wir gerade sprechen, ist sehr wichtig. (über)","a":["über das"],"why":"*sprechen über* + accusative, *das Projekt* is neuter: *über das*."},{"type":"join","q":"Das ist meine Kollegin. Ich gehe oft mit ihr joggen.","a":["Das ist meine Kollegin, mit der ich oft joggen gehe."],"why":"*mit* + dative feminine: *mit der*. The conjugated verb *gehe* goes to the end."},{"type":"join","q":"Der Bus hat Verspätung. Ich nehme ihn jeden Morgen.","a":["Der Bus, den ich jeden Morgen nehme, hat Verspätung."],"why":"*ihn* is accusative, so *den*. The relative clause goes right after *der Bus*, with a comma on both sides."}]},"sources":[{"id":"formen","title":"grammis: Flexion der Pronomina (forms of der, die, das, denen, dessen)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/3673","note":"Table of the pronoun der/die/das, which grammis says has the same forms as the relative pronoun. As a relative pronoun the feminine and plural genitive is deren (see the page on attribute clauses)."},{"id":"rel","title":"grammis: Relativsätze mit den Pronomen der, die, das (relative clauses with der, die, das)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6867"},{"id":"attribut","title":"grammis: Erweiterung von Nominalphrasen mit Attributsätzen (gender from the noun, case from the relative clause; dessen, deren)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1643"},{"id":"komma","title":"grammis: Komma bei Nebensätzen (commas with subordinate clauses)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/rechtschreibung/6203"},{"id":"wo","title":"grammis: Relativsätze mit wo (relative clauses with wo)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6868"},{"id":"was","title":"grammis: Relativsätze mit was (relative clauses with was)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6869"},{"id":"dasoderwas","title":"grammis: Das Geheimnis, das oder was du mir verraten hast? (das or was as a relative pronoun)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/131"}]},{"id":"weil-obwohl-deshalb-trotzdem","number":5,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"weil / obwohl / deshalb / trotzdem","title_en":"Reasons, results and surprises: weil, denn, deshalb, obwohl, trotzdem","summary":"Give a reason (*weil, denn*), a result (*deshalb*) or a surprise (*obwohl, trotzdem*), with the verb in the right place.","purpose":["These words link two ideas: **why** something happens (the reason), **what follows** from it (the result), or that something happens **although** you would expect the opposite (a surprise).","You use them all the time to explain, excuse and argue: *Ich komme später, **weil** der Zug Verspätung hat.* Letters, discussions and the B1 exam all need them, with the right word order."],"logic":["First choose the **meaning**: reason, result, or surprise.","Reason and result are two sides of one link. *Ich bin krank* (reason) → *ich bleibe zu Hause* (result). **weil** and **denn** introduce the reason; **deshalb** introduces the result.","A surprise works the same way: **obwohl** introduces the obstacle (*obwohl ich krank bin*), **trotzdem** introduces the surprising result (*trotzdem gehe ich arbeiten*).","Then the **word type** decides where the verb goes. **weil** and **obwohl** send the verb to the **end**. **denn** stands outside the clause (position 0), so the normal order follows: verb in position 2.","**deshalb**, **deswegen**, **darum** and **trotzdem** are adverbs. In position 1 the **verb comes right after them**: *Deshalb **bleibe** ich zu Hause.* They can also stand in the middle: *Ich bleibe **deshalb** zu Hause.*"],"rules":[{"title":"The big picture: expected or unexpected?","text":["Normally a reason leads to the expected result: *krank → zu Hause bleiben*. Use **weil / denn** for the reason and **deshalb** for the result.","Sometimes the expected result does **not** happen: *krank → trotzdem arbeiten*. Use **obwohl** for the obstacle and **trotzdem** for the surprising result."],"table":{"caption":"Which word? What the word introduces × expected or not","head":["","expected","unexpected"],"rows":[["it introduces the cause or obstacle","**weil**, **denn**","**obwohl**"],["it introduces the result","**deshalb** (*deswegen, darum*)","**trotzdem**"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich bleibe zu Hause, **weil** ich krank bin.","en":"I'm staying at home because I'm ill."},{"de":"Ich bin krank. **Deshalb** bleibe ich zu Hause.","en":"I'm ill. That's why I'm staying at home."},{"de":"Ich gehe zur Arbeit, **obwohl** ich krank bin.","en":"I'm going to work although I'm ill."},{"de":"Ich bin krank. **Trotzdem** gehe ich zur Arbeit.","en":"I'm ill. I'm going to work anyway."}],"source":"konzessiv"},{"title":"Reason with weil: verb at the end","text":["**weil** starts a subordinate clause: the conjugated verb goes to the **end**. With two verb parts, the conjugated one goes last: *weil ich arbeiten **muss***.","The *weil*-clause can come first. Then the main clause **starts with its verb**. A *weil*-clause alone also answers a *warum?* question."],"examples":[{"de":"Ich komme heute später, **weil** der Zug Verspätung **hat**.","en":"I'm coming in later today because the train is late."},{"de":"**Weil** ich krank **bin**, **bleibe** ich zu Hause.","en":"Because I'm ill, I'm staying at home."},{"de":"Warum lernst du Deutsch? – **Weil** ich in der Schweiz arbeiten **möchte**.","en":"Why are you learning German? – Because I'd like to work in Switzerland."}],"source":"weil"},{"title":"Reason with denn: normal word order","text":"**denn** also gives a reason, but it doesn't change the word order. It stands in **position 0**, between the two sentences, so the verb stays in **position 2**, as in any normal sentence.","examples":[{"de":"Wir nehmen das Velo, **denn** die Parkplätze in der Stadt **sind** teuer.","en":"We're taking the bike, because parking in town is expensive."},{"de":"Ich komme nicht mit, **denn** ich **habe** keine Zeit.","en":"I'm not coming along, because I don't have time.","note":"The *denn* part always comes after the sentence it explains. To put the reason first, or to answer *Warum?*, use *weil*."}],"source":"denn"},{"title":"Result with deshalb, deswegen, darum","text":["**deshalb**, **deswegen** and **darum** mean the same: *that's why, so*. They introduce the **result** and refer back to the reason before them.","They are adverbs, so they count as one sentence part. In position 1 the **verb comes right after them**. They can also stand in the middle of the sentence, after the verb."],"table":{"caption":"Two places for deshalb","head":["position","example"],"rows":[["position 1, verb right after it","Der Bus hatte Verspätung. **Deshalb bin** ich zu spät gekommen."],["in the middle, after the verb","Der Bus hatte Verspätung. Ich **bin deshalb** zu spät gekommen."]]},"examples":[{"de":"Mein Kollege ist krank. **Deshalb muss** ich seine Kunden anrufen.","en":"My colleague is ill. So I have to call his customers."},{"de":"Die Miete ist hoch. Wir suchen **deswegen** eine kleinere Wohnung.","en":"The rent is high. So we're looking for a smaller flat."},{"de":"Im Zug kann ich lesen. **Darum fahre** ich lieber mit dem Zug als mit dem Auto.","en":"I can read on the train. That's why I'd rather take the train than the car."}],"source":"adverb"},{"title":"Surprise with obwohl: verb at the end","text":"**obwohl** (although) introduces the **obstacle**: the thing that should have stopped the result. It works like *weil*: verb at the **end**, and if the clause comes first, the main clause starts with its verb.","examples":[{"de":"Sie ist pünktlich gekommen, **obwohl** ihr Bus Verspätung **hatte**.","en":"She arrived on time although her bus was late."},{"de":"**Obwohl** ich seit drei Jahren in Bern **wohne**, **verstehe** ich Berndeutsch noch nicht gut.","en":"Although I've lived in Bern for three years, I still don't understand Bernese German well."}],"source":"obwohl"},{"title":"Surprise with trotzdem: verb right after it","text":"**trotzdem** (anyway, nevertheless) introduces the **surprising result**. It is an adverb, like *deshalb*: in position 1 the verb comes right after it, or it stands in the middle of the sentence.","examples":[{"de":"Der Chef war gestern krank. **Trotzdem hat** er an der Sitzung teilgenommen.","en":"The boss was ill yesterday. He still took part in the meeting."},{"de":"Ich habe wenig geschlafen. Ich bin **trotzdem** fit.","en":"I didn't sleep much. I'm fit anyway."}],"source":"trotzdem"},{"title":"trotz, obwohl, trotzdem: one idea, three word types","text":["The same surprise can be said three ways. **trotz** is a preposition and takes a **noun** in the genitive (see the topic on the genitive). **obwohl** takes a **clause** with the verb at the end. **trotzdem** starts a **main clause**, with the verb right after it.","Reasons have the same trio: **wegen** + noun, **weil** + clause, **deshalb** + main clause."],"table":{"caption":"Same meaning, different word order","head":["word","type","what follows","example"],"rows":[["**trotz**","preposition","a noun (genitive)","**Trotz des Regens** gehen wir wandern."],["**obwohl**","conjunction","a clause, verb at the end","**Obwohl** es **regnet**, gehen wir wandern."],["**trotzdem**","adverb","verb right after it","Es regnet. **Trotzdem gehen** wir wandern."]]},"examples":[{"de":"**Trotz des Regens** gehen wir wandern.","en":"Despite the rain, we're going hiking."},{"de":"**Obwohl** es regnet, gehen wir wandern.","en":"Although it's raining, we're going hiking."},{"de":"Es regnet. **Trotzdem** gehen wir wandern.","en":"It's raining. We're going hiking anyway."}],"source":"trotzdem"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"weil with the verb in position 2: spoken only","text":["In everyday speech you often hear *weil* with the verb in position 2, usually after a short pause: *…, weil – ich hab keine Zeit.* In speech this is almost as common as the verb at the end, and grammis says it is not simply wrong.","In writing it is judged as a mistake. For emails, letters and the exam, put the verb at the end."],"examples":[{"de":"Ich komme nicht mit, weil ich hab keine Zeit. (gesprochen)","en":"I'm not coming along, because I haven't got time.","note":"*hab* is the spoken short form of *habe*."},{"de":"Ich komme nicht mit, **weil** ich keine Zeit **habe**. (geschrieben)","en":"I'm not coming along, because I don't have time."}],"source":"verbstellung"},{"title":"trotzdem as a conjunction: avoid it","text":"You may read or hear *trotzdem* used like *obwohl*, with the verb at the end: *trotzdem es regnet*. grammis notes that standard German avoids this. Use *obwohl* for the clause and keep *trotzdem* as an adverb.","examples":[{"de":"Trotzdem es regnet, gehen wir wandern. (avoid)","en":"Although it's raining, we're going hiking."},{"de":"**Obwohl** es regnet, gehen wir wandern. (standard)","en":"Although it's raining, we're going hiking."}],"source":"konzessiv"},{"title":"Obwohl, … to correct yourself","text":"In speech, *obwohl* followed by a pause and normal word order can correct what you just said, like English \"Although, actually …\". This is a separate use; for joining two ideas, the verb still goes to the end.","examples":[{"de":"Ich nehme das Auto. Obwohl – mit dem Zug bin ich schneller.","en":"I'll take the car. Although, actually, I'm quicker by train."}],"source":"obwohl"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ich komme nicht, weil ich habe keine Zeit.","right":"Ich komme nicht, weil ich keine Zeit **habe**.","why":"*weil* sends the verb to the end. English *because*, French *parce que*, Italian *perché* and Spanish *porque* don't change the word order, so it's easy to forget. Verb in position 2 after *weil* is spoken only."},{"wrong":"Deshalb ich bleibe zu Hause.","right":"Deshalb **bleibe ich** zu Hause.","why":"*deshalb* takes position 1, so the verb comes next. English *so I stay* and French *c'est pourquoi je reste* put the subject first; German doesn't."},{"wrong":"Ich bin müde, trotzdem ich gehe joggen.","right":"Ich bin müde, trotzdem **gehe ich** joggen.","why":"*trotzdem* works like *deshalb*: the verb comes right after it."},{"wrong":"…, denn ich keine Zeit habe.","right":"…, denn ich **habe** keine Zeit.","why":"*denn* doesn't change the word order: the verb stays in position 2. Of the reason words, only *weil* sends it to the end."},{"wrong":"Denn ich krank war, bin ich zu Hause geblieben.","right":"**Weil** ich krank war, bin ich zu Hause geblieben.","why":"A *denn* part can't come first. To put the reason first, use *weil*."},{"wrong":"Ich gehe joggen, trotzdem ich müde bin.","right":"Ich gehe joggen, **obwohl** ich müde bin.","why":"For a clause with the verb at the end, use *obwohl*. Standard German avoids *trotzdem* as a conjunction."},{"wrong":"Obwohl es regnet, wir gehen wandern.","right":"Obwohl es regnet, **gehen wir** wandern.","why":"When the *obwohl*- or *weil*-clause comes first, the main clause starts with its verb. English and French put the subject first here, so this is a common transfer mistake."},{"wrong":"Ich habe viel gelernt. Trotzdem habe ich die Prüfung bestanden.","right":"Ich habe viel gelernt. **Deshalb** habe ich die Prüfung bestanden.","why":"Passing is the expected result of studying, so it takes *deshalb*. *trotzdem* is only for a surprise: *Ich habe wenig gelernt. Trotzdem habe ich bestanden.*"}],"examples":[{"de":"Ich rufe dich später an, **weil** ich gerade in einer Sitzung bin.","en":"I'll call you later because I'm in a meeting right now."},{"de":"Ich nehme einen Regenschirm mit, **denn** am Nachmittag soll es regnen.","en":"I'm taking an umbrella, because it's supposed to rain in the afternoon."},{"de":"Die Kita schliesst um sechs. **Deshalb** muss ich pünktlich gehen.","en":"The nursery closes at six. So I have to leave on time."},{"de":"Ich hatte Fieber. Ich bin **deswegen** nicht zur Arbeit gegangen.","en":"I had a temperature. So I didn't go to work."},{"de":"**Obwohl** die Wohnung klein ist, ist sie sehr teuer.","en":"Although the flat is small, it's very expensive."},{"de":"Er spricht gut Deutsch, **obwohl** er erst seit einem Jahr hier lebt.","en":"He speaks German well, although he has only lived here for a year."},{"de":"Das Wetter war schlecht. **Trotzdem** sind wir auf den Pilatus gefahren.","en":"The weather was bad. We went up Mount Pilatus anyway."},{"de":"Ich mag meinen Job. **Darum** bleibe ich in der Firma.","en":"I like my job. That's why I'm staying with the company."}],"later":"More linking words come later: *da* (as, since: formal, verb at the end), *daher* (= *deshalb*, more formal), *sodass* (so that: result, verb at the end), *dennoch* (= *trotzdem*, more formal) and *zwar … aber* (it's true … but).","selfcheck":{"types":{"choose":{"label":"Which word?","instruction":"Write the missing word: *weil*, *denn*, *deshalb*, *obwohl* or *trotzdem*. Write one word only. Use two clues: the meaning (reason, result or surprise) and where the verb is (at the end, in position 2, or right after the gap). Where *deshalb* fits, *deswegen* and *darum* are also right.","example":{"q":"Ich trinke viel Wasser, ___ es heiss ist.","a":"weil","why":"A reason, and the verb *ist* is at the end → *weil*."}},"join":{"label":"Join the sentences","instruction":"Join the two sentences with the word in brackets. Change the word order where the word needs it, and use a pronoun if the noun repeats. A *weil*- or *obwohl*-clause can come first or second; *denn* always joins the second sentence; *deshalb* and *trotzdem* can stand in position 1 or after the verb. Write the whole new sentence.","example":{"q":"Ich habe Hunger. Ich esse ein Sandwich. (deshalb)","a":"Ich habe Hunger, deshalb esse ich ein Sandwich.","why":"*deshalb* introduces the result, and the verb comes right after it: *esse ich*."}}},"items":[{"type":"choose","q":"Ich mache einen Deutschkurs, ___ ich die B1-Prüfung bestehen will.","a":["weil"],"why":"A reason, and the verb (*will*) is at the end → *weil*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Ich mache einen Deutschkurs, ___ ich will die B1-Prüfung bestehen.","a":["denn"],"why":"A reason, but the verb (*will*) stays in position 2 → *denn*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Ich will die B1-Prüfung bestehen. ___ mache ich einen Deutschkurs.","a":["Deshalb","Deswegen","Darum"],"why":"The course is the result, and the verb comes right after the gap → *deshalb* (or *deswegen*, *darum*)."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ es schon spät ist, arbeitet mein Chef noch.","a":["Obwohl"],"why":"It's late, so you'd expect him to stop: an obstacle. The verb (*ist*) is at the end → *obwohl*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Es ist schon spät. ___ arbeitet mein Chef noch.","a":["Trotzdem"],"why":"A surprising result, and the verb comes right after the gap → *trotzdem*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Es gab einen Stau. Ich bin ___ zu spät zur Sitzung gekommen.","a":["deshalb","deswegen","darum"],"why":"Being late is the expected result of a traffic jam → *deshalb*, here in the middle of the sentence."},{"type":"choose","q":"Er ist zur Arbeit gegangen, ___ er Fieber hatte.","a":["obwohl"],"why":"A temperature should have kept him at home: an obstacle. The verb (*hatte*) is at the end → *obwohl*."},{"type":"join","q":"Das Restaurant ist teuer. Wir essen oft dort. (trotzdem)","a":["Das Restaurant ist teuer, trotzdem essen wir oft dort.","Das Restaurant ist teuer, wir essen trotzdem oft dort.","Das Restaurant ist teuer und trotzdem essen wir oft dort.","Das Restaurant ist teuer und wir essen trotzdem oft dort.","Das Restaurant ist teuer, trotzdem essen wir dort oft.","Das Restaurant ist teuer, wir essen dort trotzdem oft.","Das Restaurant ist teuer und trotzdem essen wir dort oft.","Das Restaurant ist teuer und wir essen dort trotzdem oft.","Das Restaurant ist teuer, aber trotzdem essen wir oft dort.","Das Restaurant ist teuer, aber wir essen trotzdem oft dort."],"why":"*trotzdem* introduces the surprising result; the verb comes right after it (*essen wir*), or *trotzdem* goes in the middle."},{"type":"join","q":"Ich kann nicht kommen. Ich muss arbeiten. (weil)","a":["Ich kann nicht kommen, weil ich arbeiten muss.","Weil ich arbeiten muss, kann ich nicht kommen."],"why":"*weil* sends the conjugated verb (*muss*) to the end, after *arbeiten*."},{"type":"join","q":"Ich nehme ein Taxi. Es ist schon sehr spät. (denn)","a":["Ich nehme ein Taxi, denn es ist schon sehr spät."],"why":"*denn* keeps the normal word order: *es ist …*, verb in position 2. The *denn* part comes second."},{"type":"join","q":"Wir nehmen die Wohnung. Die Wohnung ist teuer. (obwohl)","a":["Wir nehmen die Wohnung, obwohl sie teuer ist.","Obwohl die Wohnung teuer ist, nehmen wir sie.","Obwohl sie teuer ist, nehmen wir die Wohnung.","Wir nehmen die Wohnung, obwohl die Wohnung teuer ist."],"why":"*obwohl* introduces the obstacle (*teuer*), with the verb (*ist*) at the end. If the clause comes first, the main clause starts with *nehmen*."}]},"sources":[{"id":"konzessiv","title":"grammis: Konzessivsätze (concession; trotzdem as a conjunction is avoided in standard German)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/2109"},{"id":"weil","title":"grammis connector dictionary: weil (causal subjunctor, verb at the end)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/406991"},{"id":"denn","title":"grammis connector dictionary: denn (causal, position 0, verb stays in 2nd place)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/406860"},{"id":"adverb","title":"grammis: Nicht nacherstfähige Adverbkonnektoren (deshalb, darum, trotzdem: position 1 or middle field)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1279"},{"id":"deshalb","title":"grammis connector dictionary: deshalb (consequence; synonyms daher, deswegen)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/407094"},{"id":"deswegen","title":"grammis connector dictionary: deswegen (consequence)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/407028"},{"id":"darum","title":"grammis connector dictionary: d(a)rum (consequence)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/407096"},{"id":"obwohl","title":"grammis connector dictionary: obwohl (concessive subjunctor, verb at the end)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/406906"},{"id":"trotzdem","title":"grammis connector dictionary: trotzdem (concessive adverb; compare trotz + genitive)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/konnektoren/406981"},{"id":"verbstellung","title":"grammis: Verbstellung nach weil, obwohl, wobei (verb in 2nd place in speech)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/133"}]},{"id":"passiv-praesens","number":6,"added":"2026-11-07","level":"B1.1","title":"Passiv Präsens","title_en":"The passive: what is done, not who does it","summary":"Say what happens without saying who does it (*Das Altpapier **wird** am Dienstag **abgeholt**.*), also with modal verbs (*Das Formular **muss** bis Freitag **ausgefüllt werden**.*).","purpose":["The passive puts the **action** in the centre, not the person who does it: *Das Altpapier **wird** am Dienstag **abgeholt**.* (Waste paper is collected on Tuesday.) Who collects it doesn't matter, or everyone knows.","You meet it everywhere in Switzerland: notices in the stairwell, forms, instructions, recycling calendars, SBB announcements and work emails (*Die Rechnung **wird** morgen **verschickt**.*)."],"logic":["The passive is **werden + Partizip II**. *werden* is conjugated and stands in position 2. The Partizip II goes to the **end**: *Die Heizung **wird** heute **repariert**.*","From active to passive, the **accusative object becomes the subject** (nominative): *Man repariert **den** Lift.* → **Der** *Lift wird repariert.*","The doer usually disappears. If you need it, add it with **von** + dative: *vom Hauswart*.","With a **modal verb**, the modal is in position 2, and **Partizip II + werden** go to the end: *Das Formular **muss** ausgefüllt **werden**.*","In a subordinate clause (*dass, weil, ob* …), the conjugated verb goes **last**: *…, dass das Formular ausgefüllt werden **muss**.*"],"rules":[{"title":"werden + Partizip II","text":"Conjugate *werden* for the subject and put the Partizip II at the end. The Partizip II never changes.","table":{"caption":"Passiv Präsens of fragen (to ask)","head":["","werden","example"],"rows":[["ich","**werde**","Ich **werde** oft **gefragt**."],["du","**wirst**","Du **wirst** oft **gefragt**."],["er / sie / es","**wird**","Sie **wird** oft **gefragt**."],["wir","**werden**","Wir **werden** oft **gefragt**."],["ihr","**werdet**","Ihr **werdet** oft **gefragt**."],["sie / Sie","**werden**","Sie **werden** oft **gefragt**."]]},"examples":[{"de":"Das Altpapier **wird** am Dienstag **abgeholt**.","en":"Waste paper is collected on Tuesday."},{"de":"Die Pakete **werden** morgen **geliefert**.","en":"The parcels are being delivered tomorrow."},{"de":"In diesem Büro **wird** Französisch **gesprochen**.","en":"French is spoken in this office."}],"source":"aktivpassiv"},{"title":"From active to passive: the accusative object becomes the subject","text":["Take the **accusative object** of the active sentence and make it the **subject** (nominative). *werden* agrees with this new subject: singular *wird*, plural *werden*.","The active subject disappears. If it is *man*, it always disappears. A dative object stays dative: *Man hilft **den Kunden** sofort.* → **Den Kunden** *wird sofort geholfen.*"],"examples":[{"de":"**Der Lift wird** heute **repariert**.","en":"The lift is being repaired today.","note":"Active: *Man repariert **den Lift** heute.* Accusative *den Lift* → nominative *der Lift*."},{"de":"**Die Rechnungen werden** am Monatsende **verschickt**.","en":"The invoices are sent out at the end of the month.","note":"Active: *Wir verschicken die Rechnungen am Monatsende.* Plural subject, so *werden*."}],"source":"subjekt"},{"title":"The doer with von, a means or cause with durch","text":["Most passive sentences don't name the doer. If you want to name it, use **von** + dative for the person or organisation that does it: *von der Chefin, von der Gemeinde, vom Hauswart* (*vom* = *von dem*).","For a **means** or a **cause**, use **durch** + accusative: *durch eine Wärmepumpe, durch den Lärm*."],"examples":[{"de":"Die Sitzung **wird von der Chefin geleitet**.","en":"The meeting is chaired by the boss."},{"de":"Die Lernenden **werden von einer erfahrenen Fachperson betreut**.","en":"The apprentices are supervised by an experienced professional."},{"de":"Die Wohnung **wird durch eine Wärmepumpe geheizt**.","en":"The flat is heated by a heat pump."},{"de":"Der Verkehr **wird durch die Baustelle behindert**.","en":"The traffic is held up by the roadworks."}],"source":"agens"},{"title":"Passive without a subject: Hier wird nicht geraucht.","text":["Some passive sentences have **no subject** at all. They say what people do (or don't do) in general, often with verbs used without an object: *arbeiten, rauchen, essen, tanzen, lachen*. *werden* is always **wird**.","If nothing else stands in position 1, **es** fills it: *Es wird viel gelacht.* If another word is in position 1, *es* disappears: *Bei uns wird viel gelacht.*"],"examples":[{"de":"Hier **wird** nicht **geraucht**.","en":"No smoking here."},{"de":"Am Sonntag **wird** nicht **gearbeitet**.","en":"People don't work on Sundays."},{"de":"In unserem Team **wird** viel **gelacht**.","en":"There's a lot of laughing in our team."},{"de":"**Es wird** um 12 Uhr **gegessen**.","en":"Lunch is at 12."}],"source":"subjektlos"},{"title":"Passive with modal verbs: muss … ausgefüllt werden","text":"The **modal verb** (*muss, kann, darf, soll*) is conjugated and stands in position 2. At the end come the **Partizip II** and then **werden** (the infinitive, it never changes). This is what notices and forms use all the time.","examples":[{"de":"Das Formular **muss** bis Freitag **ausgefüllt werden**.","en":"The form must be filled in by Friday."},{"de":"Velos **dürfen** hier nicht **abgestellt werden**.","en":"Bikes may not be parked here."},{"de":"Der Termin **kann** auch online **verschoben werden**.","en":"The appointment can also be moved online."},{"de":"Hunde **müssen** an der Leine **geführt werden**.","en":"Dogs must be kept on a lead."}],"source":"modal"},{"title":"In a subordinate clause the conjugated verb goes last","text":"After *dass, weil, ob, wenn* and in relative clauses, the whole verb group goes to the end, and the **conjugated** verb is the very last word: *… repariert **wird***, *… ausgefüllt werden **muss***.","examples":[{"de":"Wir informieren Sie, dass das Wasser am Montag **abgestellt wird**.","en":"We'd like to let you know that the water will be turned off on Monday."},{"de":"Weisst du, ob das Paket heute **geliefert wird**?","en":"Do you know if the parcel is being delivered today?"},{"de":"Bitte beachten Sie, dass der Antrag **unterschrieben werden muss**.","en":"Please note that the application must be signed."}],"source":"klammer"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"ist geöffnet (the state) or wird geöffnet (the action)?","text":["**sein + Partizip II** describes a **state**, the result of an action: *Der Laden **ist** geöffnet.* (The shop is open.) This is called the *Zustandspassiv*.","**werden + Partizip II** describes the **action** itself, what happens: *Die Türen **werden** um 19 Uhr **geöffnet**.* (The doors are opened at 7 pm.)"],"examples":[{"de":"Die Post **ist** am Samstag bis 12 Uhr **geöffnet**.","en":"The post office is open until 12 on Saturday.","note":"Opening hours: a state, so *ist*."},{"de":"Die Türen **werden** um 19 Uhr **geöffnet**.","en":"The doors open at 7 pm.","note":"Someone opens them at that moment: an action, so *werden*."},{"de":"Das Formular **ist** schon **ausgefüllt**.","en":"The form has already been filled in.","note":"The action is over; this is the result."}],"source":"zustand"},{"title":"werden has three jobs","text":["**werden + Partizip II** = passive: *Ich werde morgen **angerufen**.* (I'm being called tomorrow.)","**werden + infinitive** = future: *Ich werde morgen **anrufen**.* (I will call tomorrow.)","**werden** alone = to become: *Sie wird Pflegefachfrau.* (She's becoming a nurse.)","So when *werden* is the conjugated verb, look at the verb at the end: a Partizip II (*gemacht*) means passive, an infinitive (*machen*) means future."],"examples":[{"de":"Die Heizung **wird** morgen **repariert**.","en":"The heating is being repaired tomorrow.","note":"Passive: Partizip II."},{"de":"Der Techniker **wird** morgen **kommen**.","en":"The technician will come tomorrow.","note":"Future: infinitive."}],"source":"hilfsverb"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Das Formular muss werden ausgefüllt.","right":"Das Formular muss **ausgefüllt werden**.","why":"English *must be filled in*, French *doit être rempli*, Italian *deve essere compilato* and Spanish *debe ser rellenado* put \"be\" first. German puts *werden* last."},{"wrong":"Das Formular muss bis Freitag ausgefüllt.","right":"Das Formular muss bis Freitag ausgefüllt **werden**.","why":"With a modal verb the passive still needs *werden* at the end."},{"wrong":"Die Briefe sind jeden Tag verteilt.","right":"Die Briefe **werden** jeden Tag verteilt.","why":"An action that happens needs *werden*. English *are*, French *sont*, Italian *sono* and Spanish *son* lead learners to *sind*, but *sein* + Partizip II only describes a state."},{"wrong":"Die Sitzung wird bei der Chefin geleitet.","right":"Die Sitzung wird **von** der Chefin geleitet.","why":"*bei* sounds like English *by*, but it means \"at\" or \"with\". The doer takes *von*."},{"wrong":"Die Sitzung wird von die Chefin geleitet.","right":"Die Sitzung wird von **der** Chefin geleitet.","why":"*von* always takes the dative. (*durch* takes the accusative: *durch den Lärm*.)"},{"wrong":"Den Lift wird repariert.","right":"**Der** Lift wird repariert.","why":"The accusative object of the active sentence becomes the subject, so it changes to the nominative."},{"wrong":"Hier werden nicht geraucht.","right":"Hier **wird** nicht geraucht.","why":"A passive without a subject always takes *wird*, the singular."},{"wrong":"…, dass das Formular muss ausgefüllt werden.","right":"…, dass das Formular ausgefüllt werden **muss**.","why":"In a *dass*-clause the conjugated verb (the modal) goes to the very end."}],"examples":[{"de":"Glas **wird** nach Farben **getrennt**.","en":"Glass is sorted by colour."},{"de":"Die Reisenden **werden gebeten**, in Fahrtrichtung rechts auszusteigen.","en":"Passengers are asked to get off on the right in the direction of travel."},{"de":"In Schweizer Zügen **wird** nicht **geraucht**.","en":"There's no smoking on Swiss trains."},{"de":"Die Mitarbeitenden **werden** per E-Mail **informiert**.","en":"The employees are informed by email."},{"de":"Leere Batterien **können** im Laden **abgegeben werden**.","en":"Empty batteries can be handed in at the shop."},{"de":"Die Steuererklärung **muss** bis Ende März **eingereicht werden**.","en":"The tax return must be submitted by the end of March."},{"de":"Ich habe gehört, dass das Büro im Januar **renoviert wird**.","en":"I've heard that the office is being renovated in January."},{"de":"Der Laden **ist** am Sonntag **geschlossen**.","en":"The shop is closed on Sunday.","note":"A state: *sein* + Partizip II."}],"later":"**Präteritum passive**: *wurde* + Partizip II, for the past (*Das Schulhaus **wurde** 1905 **gebaut**.* – The school building was built in 1905.). You'll read it in texts about the past. The **Perfekt passive** (*ist gebaut worden*) and the passive with *bekommen* (*Ich bekomme das Paket geliefert.* – The parcel is delivered to me.) come in a later lesson.","selfcheck":{"types":{"choose":{"label":"wird, werden or ist?","instruction":"Choose the word in brackets that fits the gap. Write one word.","example":{"q":"Das Museum ___ heute bis 20 Uhr geöffnet. (wird / ist)","a":"ist","why":"Opening hours describe a state: *ist geöffnet*."}},"end":{"label":"Complete the end","instruction":"Put the verb in brackets into the passive (Präsens). Write every word that goes in the gap, in the right order. If a modal verb is in brackets, use it too, in the right form.","example":{"q":"Ich hoffe, dass mein Velo bald ___ (reparieren).","a":"repariert wird","why":"Passive: *repariert* + *wird*. In a *dass*-clause the conjugated verb goes last."}},"rewrite":{"label":"Active → passive","instruction":"Rewrite the sentence in the passive (Präsens). If the active subject is *man*, leave it out. Any other doer goes in with *von*. Write the whole sentence.","example":{"q":"Man repariert die Strasse.","a":"Die Strasse wird repariert.","why":"*die Strasse* becomes the subject; *man* disappears."}},"which":{"label":"Passive or future?","instruction":"Is *werden* used for the passive or for the future in this sentence? Write **Passiv** or **Futur**.","example":{"q":"Ich werde dich morgen anrufen.","a":"Futur","why":"*werden* + infinitive (*anrufen*) = future (**Futur**)."}}},"items":[{"type":"choose","q":"Das Altpapier ___ jeden zweiten Dienstag abgeholt. (wird / ist)","a":["wird"],"why":"Something that happens regularly is an action: *wird abgeholt*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Keine Sorge, das Formular ___ schon ausgefüllt. Ich habe es gestern gemacht. (wird / ist)","a":["ist"],"why":"The work was done yesterday; now it's a state, the result: *ist ausgefüllt*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Die Briefe ___ jeden Morgen verteilt. (wird / werden)","a":["werden"],"why":"*die Briefe* is plural, so *werden*."},{"type":"end","q":"Velos dürfen im Treppenhaus nicht ___ (abstellen).","a":["abgestellt werden"],"why":"Modal verb in position 2, then Partizip II + *werden* at the end."},{"type":"end","q":"Im Brief steht, dass die Miete bis Ende Monat ___ (bezahlen, müssen).","a":["bezahlt werden muss"],"why":"In a *dass*-clause: Partizip II, *werden*, and the conjugated modal *muss* last."},{"type":"end","q":"Weisst du, ob die Pakete heute ___ (liefern)?","a":["geliefert werden"],"why":"*die Pakete* is plural: *werden*, at the end because of *ob*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Man sammelt die Batterien im Laden.","a":["Die Batterien werden im Laden gesammelt.","Im Laden werden die Batterien gesammelt."],"why":"*die Batterien* becomes the subject (plural, so *werden*); *man* disappears."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Der Hauswart repariert die Waschmaschine.","a":["Die Waschmaschine wird vom Hauswart repariert.","Die Waschmaschine wird von dem Hauswart repariert.","Vom Hauswart wird die Waschmaschine repariert."],"why":"The doer comes back with *von* + dative: *vom Hauswart*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Man muss den Antrag online ausfüllen.","a":["Der Antrag muss online ausgefüllt werden.","Online muss der Antrag ausgefüllt werden."],"why":"*den Antrag* becomes *der Antrag* (nominative). Modal + Partizip II + *werden*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"Man arbeitet am Sonntag nicht.","a":["Am Sonntag wird nicht gearbeitet.","Es wird am Sonntag nicht gearbeitet."],"why":"No object, so no subject: *wird* + Partizip II, with *es* only if position 1 is empty."},{"type":"which","q":"Der Techniker wird am Montag kommen.","a":["Futur"],"why":"*werden* + infinitive (*kommen*) = future (**Futur**)."},{"type":"which","q":"Die Heizung wird am Montag repariert.","a":["Passiv"],"why":"*werden* + Partizip II (*repariert*) = passive (**Passiv**)."}]},"sources":[{"id":"aktivpassiv","title":"grammis: Aktiv und Passiv (active and passive: werden + Partizip II)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6723"},{"id":"subjekt","title":"grammis: Werden-Passiv mit Subjekt (the accusative object becomes the subject)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/930"},{"id":"agens","title":"grammis: Die Formulierung der Agens-Rolle in Aktiv- und Passivsätzen (the doer with von or durch)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1361"},{"id":"praep","title":"grammis: Präpositionalphrase (von + dative, durch + accusative)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/5747"},{"id":"subjektlos","title":"grammis: Subjektloses werden-Passiv (passive without a subject)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1068"},{"id":"modal","title":"grammis: Fehlende Passivierbarkeit (modal verbs with a passive infinitive)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/1616"},{"id":"klammer","title":"grammis: Wortstellung in der Satzklammer (verb order at the end of the clause)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6840"},{"id":"zustand","title":"grammis: Vertiefung: sein-Passiv (the state: ist geöffnet)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/6724"},{"id":"hilfsverb","title":"grammis: Hilfsverben im Verbalkomplex (werden for the future and for the passive)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/5856"},{"id":"werdenpassiv","title":"grammis: Werden-Passiv (forms in all tenses, e.g. wurde verhaftet)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/929"}]},{"id":"genitiv","number":7,"added":"2026-11-06","level":"B1.1","title":"Genitiv","title_en":"The genitive case: whose is it?","summary":"Show who something belongs to (*das Büro des Chefs*) and use *wegen, trotz, während, statt* in writing.","purpose":["The genitive shows that something **belongs to** someone or something: *das Büro **des Chefs*** (the boss's office), *der Sitz **der Firma*** (the company's headquarters).","In everyday speech, people often say *von* + dative instead: *das Büro **vom Chef***. You still need the genitive for reading, emails, formal speech and the B1 exam, and after a few prepositions such as *wegen* (because of) and *trotz* (despite)."],"logic":["The genitive phrase comes **after** the noun it describes: *das Auto **meiner Schwester*** (literally: the car of my sister). English *'s* goes the other way round.","Only **names** go in front: *Annas Wohnung* (Anna's flat). There is no apostrophe.","The signal sits mainly in the **article**. Masculine and neuter take **des / eines**, and the noun adds **-s** or **-es**. Feminine and plural take **der**, and the noun stays unchanged.","So *der* now has a third job: masculine nominative, feminine dative and **feminine or plural genitive**. The position in the sentence tells you which.","The question word is **wessen?** (whose?). In speech, *wem gehört …?* (who does … belong to?) is more common."],"rules":[{"title":"Article words in the genitive","text":"Learn the four columns as a pattern: **des / des / der / der**. Possessives (*mein, dein, unser* …) and *kein* follow *ein*. The plural has no indefinite article, so you use *von* + dative instead (see Exceptions).","table":{"caption":"Genitive forms","head":["","masculine","neuter","feminine","plural"],"rows":[["definite","**des** Chef**s**","**des** Kind**es**","**der** Firma","**der** Kunden"],["indefinite","**eines** Chef**s**","**eines** Kind**es**","**einer** Firma","—"],["possessive","**meines** Chef**s**","**meines** Kind**es**","**meiner** Firma","**meiner** Kunden"],["negative","**keines** Chef**s**","**keines** Kind**es**","**keiner** Firma","**keiner** Kunden"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Das ist das Büro **des Chefs**.","en":"That is the boss's office."},{"de":"Die Mitarbeitenden **der Firma** arbeiten oft im Homeoffice.","en":"The company's employees often work from home."},{"de":"Die Wünsche **unserer Kunden** sind uns wichtig.","en":"Our customers' wishes are important to us."}],"source":"kasus"},{"title":"Masculine and neuter nouns add -s or -es","text":["Use **-es** after an s-sound (*-s, -ss, -z, -tz, -x*): *des Hauses, des Platzes, des Fusses*. Nouns ending in *-nis* double the s: *des Ergebnisses*.","Use **-s** after *-el, -en, -er, -chen, -ling* and after a vowel: *des Computers, des Mädchens, des Autos*.","Many one-syllable nouns allow both, and **-es** sounds more formal: *des Tages* (much more common than *des Tags*), *des Kindes* or *des Kinds*."],"examples":[{"de":"Am Ende **des Monats** bekomme ich meinen Lohn.","en":"I get my wages at the end of the month."},{"de":"Die Ergebnisse **des Projekts** sind sehr gut.","en":"The project's results are very good.","note":"*des Projektes* is also correct."},{"de":"Die Miete **des Hauses** ist gestiegen.","en":"The rent for the house has gone up."}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Weak masculine nouns take -n or -en, never -s","text":"A group of masculine nouns, almost all people or animals, end in **-(e)n** in every case except the nominative: *der Kollege → des Kollegen*. Typical endings are *-e* (*Kollege, Kunde, Junge*), *-ent* and *-ant* (*Student, Praktikant*), *-ist* (*Polizist*), plus *Mensch, Herr* (*des Herrn*) and *Nachbar*.","examples":[{"de":"Ich habe die Telefonnummer **des Kunden** nicht.","en":"I don't have the customer's phone number."},{"de":"Das ist der Laptop **meines Kollegen**.","en":"That is my colleague's laptop."}],"source":"weak"},{"title":"Names go in front and add -s","text":"Put the name before the noun and add **-s**, with no apostrophe: *Peters Büro*. If the name already ends in an s-sound (*-s, -ss, -z, -x*), write only an apostrophe: *Thomas' Auto*, *Max' Fahrrad*. The noun after the name takes no article.","examples":[{"de":"**Wessen** Jacke ist das? – Das ist **Marias** Jacke.","en":"Whose jacket is that? – It's Maria's jacket."},{"de":"**Thomas'** Präsentation war am besten.","en":"Thomas's presentation was the best."}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Prepositions with the genitive","text":"These prepositions take the genitive in writing: **wegen** (because of), **trotz** (despite), **während** (during), **statt** (instead of), **innerhalb** (within, inside) and **ausserhalb** (outside of).","examples":[{"de":"**Wegen einer Störung** fahren heute keine Züge nach Bern.","en":"Because of a fault, no trains are running to Bern today."},{"de":"**Trotz des schlechten Wetters** gehen wir am Wochenende wandern.","en":"Despite the bad weather, we're going hiking at the weekend.","note":"After an article, adjectives in the genitive end in **-en**: *des schlechten Wetters*."},{"de":"**Während der Sitzung** bitte keine Handys.","en":"No mobile phones during the meeting, please."},{"de":"**Statt eines Taxis** nehmen wir das Tram.","en":"Instead of a taxi, we'll take the tram."},{"de":"**Innerhalb einer Woche** bekommen Sie eine Antwort.","en":"You'll get an answer within a week."}],"source":"prep"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"wegen + dative in speech","text":["In speech, many people say *wegen **dem** Regen* instead of *wegen **des** Regens*. It is widespread and hardly counted as an error in speech, but in writing *wegen des Regens* is still far more common and is the safe choice. *Trotz* keeps the genitive more firmly.","With pronouns, the dative is normal: *wegen mir* (because of me). The old form *meinetwegen* now mostly means \"fine by me\"."],"examples":[{"de":"Wegen **dem** Regen sind wir zu Hause geblieben. (gesprochen)","en":"Because of the rain, we stayed at home."},{"de":"Wegen **des Regens** sind wir zu Hause geblieben. (geschrieben)","en":"Because of the rain, we stayed at home."},{"de":"Kommst du **wegen mir**? – Ja, **meinetwegen**.","en":"Are you coming because of me? – Yes, fine by me.","note":"Here *meinetwegen* means \"fine by me\"."}],"source":"wegen"},{"title":"Plural without an article: use von + dative","text":"A plural noun with no article or adjective shows no genitive at all, so German uses *von* + dative instead.","examples":[{"de":"die Meinung **von Experten**","en":"the opinion of experts"},{"de":"Die Preise **von Wohnungen** in Zürich sind sehr hoch.","en":"The prices of flats in Zurich are very high."}],"source":"wegen"},{"title":"Some nouns add -ns","text":"A few masculine nouns ending in *-e* add **-ns** in the genitive: *der Name → des Namens*, *der Gedanke → des Gedankens*, *der Glaube → des Glaubens*.","examples":[{"de":"Die Schreibweise **des Namens** ist falsch.","en":"The spelling of the name is wrong."}],"source":"weak"},{"title":"Some foreign words and abbreviations take no ending","text":"Foreign words with an unstressed ending in *-us* or *-as* usually stay unchanged: *des Zirkus, des Atlas*. Abbreviations can go without an ending: *des LKW* or *des LKWs*. Never use an apostrophe for a missing ending: *des Atlas'* is wrong.","examples":[{"de":"die Fahrerin **des LKW**","en":"the lorry's driver"}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Feminine nouns never take an ending","text":"In the genitive only the article changes for feminine nouns: *die Firma → der Firma*, never *der Firmas*.","examples":[{"de":"die Adresse **der Bank**","en":"the bank's address"}],"source":"kasus"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"das Büro der Chef","right":"das Büro **des Chefs**","why":"Masculine: *des* plus *-s* on the noun."},{"wrong":"Anna's Wohnung","right":"**Annas** Wohnung","why":"German has no apostrophe before the *-s*. The apostrophe is an English habit."},{"wrong":"des Kollegens","right":"des **Kollegen**","why":"Weak nouns take *-n*, never *-s*."},{"wrong":"der Firmas","right":"der **Firma**","why":"Feminine nouns never take a genitive ending."},{"wrong":"mein Bruders Auto","right":"das Auto **meines Bruders**","why":"Only names go before the noun. Everything else follows it, unlike English *my brother's car*."},{"wrong":"trotz dem Regen (in einem E-Mail)","right":"trotz **des Regens**","why":"In writing, use the genitive after *trotz* and *wegen*."},{"wrong":"Wegen ich krank bin, bleibe ich zu Hause.","right":"**Weil** ich krank bin, bleibe ich zu Hause.","why":"*wegen* is a preposition: a noun follows (*wegen **meiner Krankheit***). Before a clause with a verb, use *weil*."},{"wrong":"Trotz es regnet, gehen wir wandern.","right":"**Obwohl** es regnet, … / **Trotz des Regens** …","why":"*trotz* takes a noun in the genitive. Before a clause with a verb, use *obwohl*."},{"wrong":"trotz des schlechtes Wetters","right":"trotz des **schlechten** Wetters","why":"After *des* or *der* in the genitive, the adjective always ends in *-en*."}],"examples":[{"de":"Der Sitz **der Firma** ist in Zürich.","en":"The company's headquarters is in Zurich."},{"de":"Kennst du die Freundin **meines Bruders**?","en":"Do you know my brother's girlfriend?"},{"de":"Die Farbe **des Autos** gefällt mir nicht.","en":"I don't like the colour of the car."},{"de":"**Wegen der Baustelle** brauche ich zehn Minuten länger.","en":"Because of the roadworks, I need ten minutes longer."},{"de":"**Während der Ferien** ist das Büro geschlossen.","en":"The office is closed during the holidays."},{"de":"Der Name **des Hotels** ist schwierig.","en":"The hotel's name is difficult."},{"de":"Wir wohnen **ausserhalb der Stadt**.","en":"We live outside the city."},{"de":"Das ist der Hund **unserer Nachbarn**.","en":"That is our neighbours' dog."}],"later":"The genitive with adjectives in full (*trotz starken Regens*, *wegen guter Arbeit*) comes with the topic on adjective endings.","selfcheck":{"types":{"fill":{"label":"Fill the gap","instruction":"Put the words in brackets into the genitive. Write the whole phrase: article word plus noun.","example":{"q":"Das ist das Handy ___ (mein Vater).","a":"meines Vaters","why":"Masculine: *meines* plus *-s* on the noun."}},"rewrite":{"label":"Spoken → written","instruction":"Rewrite the spoken phrase with *von* or the dative in the written genitive.","example":{"q":"die Tür von dem Zimmer","a":"die Tür des Zimmers","why":"Neuter: *des* plus *-s*."}},"choose":{"label":"Preposition or conjunction?","instruction":"Choose the word in brackets that fits and write only that word. A noun follows a preposition (*wegen, trotz*); a clause with a verb at the end follows a conjunction (*weil, obwohl*).","example":{"q":"Ich bleibe heute zu Hause, ___ es regnet. (wegen / weil)","a":"weil","why":"A clause with a verb (*regnet*) follows, so you need the conjunction *weil*."}}},"items":[{"type":"fill","q":"Die Adresse ___ (die Schule) steht auf der Website.","a":["der Schule"],"why":"Feminine: *der*, with no ending on the noun."},{"type":"fill","q":"Das ist der Schreibtisch ___ (unser Praktikant).","a":["unseres Praktikanten"],"why":"*Praktikant* is a weak noun, so it takes *-en*, not *-s*."},{"type":"fill","q":"___ (wegen / der Regen) bleiben wir heute zu Hause.","a":["Wegen des Regens"],"why":"*wegen* + genitive in writing. *der Regen* is masculine: *des Regens*. In speech you'd also hear *wegen dem Regen*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Ich lese den Bericht ___ (das Team).","a":["des Teams"],"why":"Neuter: *des* plus *-s*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"die Meinung von meiner Chefin","a":["die Meinung meiner Chefin"],"why":"Feminine possessive: *meiner*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"während dem Essen","a":["während des Essens"],"why":"*während* takes the genitive in writing. *das Essen* is neuter: *des Essens*."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ des Streiks fahren heute keine Busse. (Wegen / Weil)","a":["Wegen"],"why":"A noun in the genitive follows (*des Streiks*), so you need the preposition *wegen*."},{"type":"choose","q":"Ich komme etwas später, ___ ich noch einen Termin habe. (wegen / weil)","a":["weil"],"why":"A clause with the verb at the end follows, so you need *weil*."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ es kalt ist, gehen wir im See schwimmen. (Trotz / Obwohl)","a":["Obwohl"],"why":"A clause with a verb follows, so you need the conjunction *obwohl*."},{"type":"choose","q":"___ der Kälte gehen wir im See schwimmen. (Trotz / Obwohl)","a":["Trotz"],"why":"A noun in the genitive follows (*der Kälte*), so you need the preposition *trotz*."}]},"sources":[{"id":"kasus","title":"grammis: Kasus (case)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/vggf/2218"},{"id":"s-es","title":"grammis: Eines Tags oder eines Tages? Genitive forms short and long","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/26"},{"id":"weak","title":"grammis: Autor, Doktor, Friede, Funke (weak and mixed nouns)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/28"},{"id":"prep","title":"grammis: Präpositionalphrase (prepositions and the cases they take)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/5747"},{"id":"wegen","title":"grammis: Wegen dem Regen oder wegen des Regens? Dative or genitive","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/67"}]},{"id":"konjunktiv-2","number":8,"added":"2026-11-06","level":"B1.1","title":"Konjunktiv II","title_en":"The subjunctive: polite, wishful and unreal","summary":"Be polite (*Könnten Sie …?*), give advice (*Du solltest …*), make wishes and talk about what isn't real (*Wenn ich Zeit hätte, würde ich …*).","purpose":["The normal verb forms (the indicative) describe what **is** real: *Ich habe Zeit.* (I have time.) The Konjunktiv II describes what is **not** real, or not yet real: *Wenn ich Zeit **hätte**, …* (If I had time, …).","You also use it every day to sound **polite** and **less direct**: *Ich **hätte** gern einen Kaffee.* (I'd like a coffee.) *Könnten Sie mir helfen?* (Could you help me?) It roughly matches English *would*, *could* and *should*."],"logic":["Most verbs use **würde + infinitive**, the same pattern as a modal verb: *würde* in position 2, the infinitive at the end. *Ich **würde** gern in Bern **wohnen**.*","A few very common verbs have their **own one-word form**, built from the Präteritum with an umlaut: *war → **wäre***, *hatte → **hätte***, *konnte → **könnte***. Use these instead of *würde … sein / haben*.","The endings are the same for all of them: *ich -e, du -est, er/sie/es -e, wir -en, ihr -et, sie/Sie -en*.","For the **past**, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II, as in the Perfekt: *Ich **hätte** dich **angerufen**.* (I would have called you.)"],"rules":[{"title":"The one-word forms: wäre, hätte, würde, könnte","text":"Take the Präteritum (*war, hatte, wurde, konnte*), add an umlaut, and use the *-e* endings.","table":{"caption":"Konjunktiv II of the four key verbs","head":["","sein","haben","werden","können"],"rows":[["ich","**wäre**","**hätte**","**würde**","**könnte**"],["du","wär(e)st","hättest","würdest","könntest"],["er / sie / es","wäre","hätte","würde","könnte"],["wir","wären","hätten","würden","könnten"],["ihr","wär(e)t","hättet","würdet","könntet"],["sie / Sie","wären","hätten","würden","könnten"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich **wäre** jetzt gern in den Bergen.","en":"I'd like to be in the mountains right now."},{"de":"Wir **hätten** gern mehr Ferien.","en":"We'd like more holidays."}],"source":"k2"},{"title":"würde + infinitive for most other verbs","text":"*würde* goes in position 2 and the infinitive goes to the end, the same verb bracket as with *können* or *müssen*.","examples":[{"de":"Ich **würde** gern mehr **reisen**.","en":"I'd like to travel more."},{"de":"An deiner Stelle **würde** ich mit der Chefin **sprechen**.","en":"If I were you, I'd talk to the boss."}],"source":"flexion"},{"title":"Modal verbs: könnte, müsste, dürfte, möchte","text":"Modal verbs take an umlaut in the Konjunktiv II: *könnte* (could), *müsste* (would have to), *dürfte* (might, would be allowed to), *möchte* (would like). **Except** *sollte* (should) and *wollte* (would want): they have no umlaut, so they look the same as the Präteritum.","examples":[{"de":"**Dürfte** ich kurz stören?","en":"Might I interrupt for a moment?"},{"de":"Du **solltest** mehr Pausen machen.","en":"You should take more breaks."},{"de":"Eigentlich **müsste** ich heute noch einkaufen.","en":"Actually, I ought to go shopping today."}],"source":"modal"},{"title":"Four uses: polite, advice, wishes, unreal conditions","text":["**Polite requests:** *Könnten Sie …? Würden Sie …? Hätten Sie …? Ich hätte gern …*","**Advice:** *Du solltest … An deiner Stelle würde ich …*","**Wishes:** *Ich wäre gern … Ich hätte gern … Wenn ich doch mehr Zeit hätte!*","**Unreal conditions:** *Wenn ich mehr Geld hätte, würde ich weniger arbeiten.*"],"examples":[{"de":"**Könnten** Sie mir bitte die Rechnung schicken?","en":"Could you send me the invoice, please?"},{"de":"**Würden** Sie das bitte noch einmal erklären?","en":"Would you explain that again, please?"},{"de":"Wenn ich doch mehr Zeit **hätte**!","en":"If only I had more time!"}],"source":"k2"},{"title":"wenn-sentences: verb at the end, then verb first","text":"In the *wenn*-clause the conjugated verb goes to the **end**. If the *wenn*-clause comes first, the main clause **starts with the verb**: *Wenn ich Zeit hätte, **würde** ich kommen.*","examples":[{"de":"Wenn ich mehr Zeit **hätte**, **würde** ich einen Abendkurs **besuchen**.","en":"If I had more time, I'd take an evening class."},{"de":"Ich **würde** mitkommen, wenn ich nicht arbeiten **müsste**.","en":"I'd come along if I didn't have to work."}],"source":"flexion"},{"title":"The past: hätte / wäre + Partizip II","text":"To talk about something in the past that didn't happen, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II. The choice between *hätte* and *wäre* is the same as in the Perfekt (*haben* or *sein*).","examples":[{"de":"Wenn der Zug pünktlich **gewesen wäre**, **hätte** ich das Meeting nicht **verpasst**.","en":"If the train had been on time, I wouldn't have missed the meeting."},{"de":"Das **hätte** ich nicht **gedacht**.","en":"I wouldn't have thought that."}],"source":"k2"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"Regular verbs always use würde","text":"For regular (weak) verbs, the one-word Konjunktiv II looks exactly like the Präteritum: *ich arbeitete* can mean \"I worked\" or \"I would work\". To be clear, use **würde**: *Wenn ich weniger arbeiten **würde**, …*","examples":[{"de":"Wenn er mehr **lernen würde**, **hätte** er bessere Noten.","en":"If he studied more, he'd get better marks."}],"source":"synkretismus"},{"title":"Some one-word forms you'll read and hear","text":"Strong verbs also have one-word forms. Most sound formal (*käme, ginge*), but a few are common in speech too: *es **gäbe*** (there would be), *ich **wüsste*** (I would know), *ich **bräuchte*** (I would need).","examples":[{"de":"**Wüssten** Sie, wo das Sitzungszimmer ist?","en":"Would you know where the meeting room is?"},{"de":"Es **wäre** gut, wenn es mehr Parkplätze **gäbe**.","en":"It would be good if there were more parking spaces."}],"source":"flexion"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ich würde gern einen Kaffee haben.","right":"Ich **hätte** gern einen Kaffee.","why":"With *haben* and *sein*, use *hätte* and *wäre*, not *würde … haben / sein*. The *würde* version isn't wrong, but it sounds clumsy."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich Zeit hätte, ich würde kommen.","right":"Wenn ich Zeit hätte, **würde ich** kommen.","why":"After a *wenn*-clause, the main clause starts with the verb."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich würde Zeit haben, …","right":"Wenn ich Zeit **hätte**, …","why":"In the *wenn*-clause the verb goes to the end, and *haben* uses *hätte*."},{"wrong":"Ich könnte gestern nicht kommen.","right":"Ich **konnte** gestern nicht kommen.","why":"*konnte* (without umlaut) = was able to. *könnte* = could, would be able to. The umlaut changes the meaning."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich das gewusst würde, …","right":"Wenn ich das gewusst **hätte**, …","why":"For the past, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich mehr Geld habe, würde ich weniger arbeiten.","right":"Wenn ich mehr Geld **hätte**, würde ich weniger arbeiten.","why":"If the situation is unreal, both parts are unreal. English (*if I had*), French (*si j'avais*), Italian (*se avessi*) and Spanish (*si tuviera*) also change the verb in the if-part."},{"wrong":"Ich hätte gestern gekommen.","right":"Ich **wäre** gestern gekommen.","why":"In the past, *hätte* or *wäre* follows the Perfekt: *ich bin gekommen* → *ich wäre gekommen*. English uses *have* for every verb (*I would have come*), German doesn't."},{"wrong":"Du söllst mehr schlafen.","right":"Du **solltest** mehr schlafen.","why":"*sollen* and *wollen* take no umlaut in the Konjunktiv II: *sollte*, *wollte*."}],"examples":[{"de":"Ich **hätte** gern einen Termin am Dienstag.","en":"I'd like an appointment on Tuesday."},{"de":"**Könntest** du mir kurz helfen?","en":"Could you help me for a moment?"},{"de":"Es **wäre** schön, wenn wir uns bald **sehen würden**.","en":"It would be nice if we saw each other soon."},{"de":"An deiner Stelle **würde** ich das Angebot **annehmen**.","en":"If I were you, I'd accept the offer."},{"de":"Ich **wäre** lieber Lehrerin geworden.","en":"I would rather have become a teacher."},{"de":"Wenn ich in Zürich **wohnen würde**, **müsste** ich nicht so lange pendeln.","en":"If I lived in Zurich, I wouldn't have to commute so far."},{"de":"**Hätten** Sie morgen Zeit für ein kurzes Gespräch?","en":"Would you have time for a short talk tomorrow?"},{"de":"Wir **sollten** früher losfahren.","en":"We should leave earlier."}],"later":"**Konjunktiv I** (*er sei, sie habe*) is used for reported speech, mainly in the news and formal texts. You only need to recognise it later, at B2.","selfcheck":{"types":{"fill":{"label":"Fill the gap","instruction":"Put the verb in brackets into the Konjunktiv II. Write one word.","example":{"q":"Ich ___ gern Urlaub. (haben)","a":"hätte","why":"*haben* → *hätte*, one word, no *würde*."}},"polite":{"label":"Make it polite","instruction":"Turn the direct command into a polite question with *Könnten Sie* or *Würden Sie*. Write the whole sentence. *bitte* is optional.","example":{"q":"Schicken Sie mir den Plan.","a":"Könnten Sie mir bitte den Plan schicken?","why":"*Könnten* comes first, and the infinitive goes to the end."}},"real":{"label":"Real or unreal?","instruction":"Choose the verb in brackets that fits and write only that word. Real or possible things use the normal form (*habe, konnte*); unreal, polite or wished-for things use the Konjunktiv II (*hätte, könnte*).","example":{"q":"Wenn es morgen regnet, ___ wir zu Hause. (bleiben / blieben)","a":"bleiben","why":"Rain tomorrow is possible, so both parts use the normal present tense."}}},"items":[{"type":"fill","q":"Wenn ich du ___, würde ich das Angebot annehmen. (sein)","a":["wäre"],"why":"*sein* → *wäre*. In the *wenn*-clause the verb goes to the end."},{"type":"fill","q":"___ Sie mir bitte helfen? (können)","a":["Könnten"],"why":"Polite question: *Könnten Sie …?*"},{"type":"fill","q":"Du ___ zum Arzt gehen. (sollen)","a":["solltest"],"why":"Advice with *sollen*: *du solltest*, with no umlaut."},{"type":"fill","q":"Wir ___ gern ein grösseres Büro. (haben)","a":["hätten"],"why":"*wir hätten*, with the *-en* ending for *wir*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Wenn ich früher aufgestanden ___, hätte ich den Bus erreicht. (sein)","a":["wäre"],"why":"Past: *aufstehen* forms its Perfekt with *sein*, so *wäre aufgestanden*."},{"type":"polite","q":"Geben Sie mir das Formular.","a":["Könnten Sie mir das Formular geben?","Könnten Sie mir bitte das Formular geben?","Könnten Sie mir das Formular bitte geben?","Würden Sie mir das Formular geben?","Würden Sie mir bitte das Formular geben?","Würden Sie mir das Formular bitte geben?"],"why":"*Könnten Sie* or *Würden Sie* first, with *geben* at the end."},{"type":"real","q":"Wenn ich morgen Zeit ___, komme ich vorbei. (habe / hätte)","a":["habe"],"why":"A real possibility: the main clause uses the normal form (*komme*), so the *wenn*-clause does too."},{"type":"real","q":"Wenn ich mehr Zeit ___, würde ich öfter Sport machen. (habe / hätte)","a":["hätte"],"why":"Unreal: the main clause has *würde*, so the *wenn*-clause needs *hätte*."},{"type":"real","q":"Gestern ___ ich nicht kommen, weil ich krank war. (konnte / könnte)","a":["konnte"],"why":"A real fact in the past: Präteritum *konnte*, without umlaut."},{"type":"real","q":"___ Sie mir bitte das Salz geben? (Konnten / Könnten)","a":["Könnten"],"why":"A polite request: Konjunktiv II *Könnten*, with umlaut."}]},"sources":[{"id":"k2","title":"grammis: Konjunktiv II","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/terminologie/401"},{"id":"flexion","title":"grammis: Verbflexion nach Tempus und Modus (verb forms by tense and mood)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/4047"},{"id":"modal","title":"grammis: Modalverb (modal verbs)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/5202"},{"id":"synkretismus","title":"grammis: Formensynkretismus von Konjunktiv und Indikativ (when subjunctive and indicative look the same)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/315"}]}];
